{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_end","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Read §2.4 of the specification, and check the relevant sections of the design document and the test files. Also, I need to compare against the base commit — but the shell is blocked. Still, I can infer what changed from the author's report, but I should read the current test files and the design document.\n\nKey spots to scrutinize:\n\n1. `link-step!`:\n```clojure\n(swap! link assoc :now now)\n(drain! link)\n(let [{:keys [outstanding pending channel-gone?]} @link\n      overdue (when-not channel-gone?\n                (->> (concat outstanding pending)\n                     (filter (fn [[_ e]] (some-> (:deadline e) (<= now))))\n                     (sort-by key)\n                     first))]\n  (when-some [[id e] overdue]\n    (emit! ...)\n    (channel-loss! link))\n  {:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? (:channel-gone? @link)\n   :dao.stream.remote/expired (first overdue)})\n```\n\nConcerns:\n- `now` could be nil (dial-step! 1-arity → link isn't stepped; but `:step` is exposed — what if the caller calls `(step cd nil)`?). `link-step!` with nil `now`: `(swap! link assoc :now nil)` — this erases the previously recorded `now`! Then the filter `(some-> (:deadline e) (<= now))` — `(<= deadline nil)` throws. Actually, `some->` only guards nil on the deadline side, not on the `now` side. `(<= 10 nil)` throws NPE/ClassCastException. So `(step cd nil)` throws. Is that reachable? `dial-step!` 2-arity guards: `(when (and (some? now) (:step! ch)) ...)`. So ws_project never steps with nil. But `links`'s `:step` is public — a driver might call it with nil. The spec says \"without a stepped `now` nothing expires,\" but stepping with nil isn't specified. This is a P3 robustness issue: `link-step!` should guard nil `now` (don't record, don't scan) or throw a composition error. Also, `(swap! link assoc :now now)` with nil overwrites a previously valid now — could this affect `new-deadline` stamping? Actually, `new-deadline` checks `(some? now)`, so a nil now makes subsequent sends unstamped. Minor.\n\n- Sort by key: `sort-by key` on ids — ids are integers, fine. `(concat outstanding pending)` — both maps keyed by id; could ids collide between outstanding and pending? A request id is either in outstanding or pending, not both (send-request! removes pending when accepted). Actually, if an accepted probe is registered, pending gets dissoc'd. So they're disjoint. Fine.\n\n- `expired (first overdue)` — overdue is the `[id e]` pair itself (first of sorted seq), so `(first overdue)` = id. Correct.\n\n- Order: first emit `channel-expired`, then `channel-loss!` emits `append-unknown`. Matches spec.\n\n- After channel-loss!, `:pending` remains (author note 2). Memory: pending entries retained but drain! early-returns when gone; close! clears pending for that reflection. If a reflection never closes, pending probes persist forever. This is bounded by max-outstanding. Spec-wise inert — P4 note.\n\n- Does the step also scan deadlines when `channel-gone?` is already true from an earlier `end`? Guarded by `when-not channel-gone?`. Good.\n\n2. Deadline stamping:\n- `send-request!`: computes the deadline before the cond. `(if-some [kept (get (:pending @link) id)] (:deadline kept) (new-deadline link))`. Note: for a kept probe that is now being sent successfully, the deadline carries over from pending — good (no re-stamp). But what about a kept probe that is later refused (full) again? Then it re-enters the pending branch: `(cond-> {:req req :reflection refl} (some? deadline) (assoc :deadline deadline))` — since kept was found, deadline comes from kept. Good.\n\n  Edge case: `admit-probe!` — the probe that waits on reflection (stored in `:probe`, no pending entry). When admitted via `admit-probe!` → `send-request!`: no pending entry → `new-deadline` at the time of admission. Matches spec (\"waiting probes are unstamped until admitted\"). Good.\n\n- `refl-append`: `(register! link refl req (new-deadline link))` — stamped at accept time. Good.\n\n- `send-named!`: stamps `(new-deadline link)` but calls it twice: `(cond-> ... (some? (new-deadline link)) (assoc :deadline (new-deadline link)))`. Both calls read `@link` — same state, no side effects, so same value. Minor inefficiency, no correctness issue. Could be P4 style. Actually, wait — is there any possibility `:now` changes between the two calls? Single-threaded driver discipline; no. P4.\n\n- Resend: `count-ask!` re-appends `(:req entry)` and resets asks; doesn't touch deadline. Good. But note: `count-ask!`'s resend calls `(stream/append! (:writer @link) ...)` directly, bypassing `room?`/`send!`. That's S2b behavior, pre-existing. Hmm, count-ask! doesn't check max-outstanding — but a resend doesn't add a new outstanding entry, so it's fine.\n\n3. `drain!` inside `link-step!`: drain! does count-ask! and retry-pending! for descriptor probes. Then step scans for overdue. If drain! observed `end`, channel-loss! already ran, and channel-gone? is true → skips scan. Good.\n\nAlso: the deadline filter uses `<=` now — spec says `:deadline <= now`. Matches.\n\n4. ws_project's `dial-step!`:\n```clojure\n([dial now]\n (when (some? now) (swap! dial assoc :now now))\n (let [{:keys [...] :as d} @dial ch @channel]\n   (when-some [project (:project ch)] (step! project step-budget))\n   (let [stepped (when (and (some? now) (:step! ch)) ((:step! ch) (:cd ch) now))]\n     (when-some [handle (:handle ch)]\n       (reset! mirror-cursor (remote/mirror-step ...))\n       (when (and (:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? stepped) (stream/closable? handle))\n         (stream/close! handle))))\n   @mirror-cursor))\n```\n\nConcerns:\n- `now` validation: no check that `now` is a number. `(dial-step! dial \"x\")` → link's `:now` is \"x\", deadline `(<= 10 \"x\")` throws. Well, the driver controls it; the spec doesn't require validation. P4.\n\n- Ordering issue: projection step first, then link step, then mirror step. Wait — in dial-step!, the mirror step is over the ring for requests coming in the other direction. The link step drains the link's reader (which is the ring? no wait). Let me check: in `establish!`, the link's channel end is `{:reader ring :writer handle}`. The projection appends ws payload to the ring. The mirror also reads from the ring (`mirror-cursor` on `ring`). Both the link's `drain!` and `mirror-step` read the same ring at different cursors. Both directions of traffic share one ring? Hmm — that's the existing architecture (S0/S1): the ring carries deposited ws payload; both the link (as asker, reading responses) and the mirror (as answerer, reading requests) scan it, each ignoring values that aren't for them (link drops non-responses, mirror drops non-requests). OK, pre-existing.\n\n- Double-drain concern: `dial-step!` with `now` calls the link's `:step`, which internally calls `drain!`. But the reflection ops also call `drain!`. Since drain! is idempotent w.r.t. cursor (reads until blocked or budget exhausted), there's no double-read issue; budget: each drain! call can read up to drain-budget values. Link step + subsequent reflection op drain = 2×budget per tick. Existing behavior (any number of op calls each drain). The step adds one more budgeted drain per tick — bounded. Fine.\n\n- Mirror step runs even if the channel is gone (post-expiry): mirror-step over a closed ring... ring isn't closed yet — the handle close happens later, and the host deposits :ws/closed, then projection closes the ring. In this tick, mirror-step still runs once. Fine.\n\n- Handle close: `(stream/close! handle)` — idempotent per ws. Good.\n\n- Note: `(:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? stepped)` — when stepped is nil (no channel or no now), nil → falsy. Good.\n\n- `establish!`: `(when (some? now) ((:step ls) cd now))` before reset! channel. At this point the link has just been created via `remote/links`. The attach probe was sent when... wait — when is the attach probe sent? `dial-attach!` calls `establish!`, then `((:reflect! @channel) descriptor)`. `reflect!` is the `:attach` fn of `links`, which creates the link via `link-for!` and calls `send-request!` with the descriptor probe. Hmm — but `establish!` calls `(:step ls) cd now` before any attach — that creates the link via `link-for!` (a fresh link) and steps it, recording `:now`. Then `(:attach ls)`'s descriptor → `link-for! cd` returns the same (existing) link → `send-request!` → `new-deadline` uses the recorded `now`. So the probe gets stamped. That's the rationale for stepping in establish!. Correct.\n\n  But wait: `dial-resolve!` also calls `establish!` and then resolve. Also fine.\n\n  One subtle point: in `establish!`, `now` comes from `@dial` — the dial's last stepped now. What if the dial has never been stepped with a now? nil → no step → no deadline. Fine.\n\n5. `links`'s `:step` — `(fn [cd now] (when (contains? channels cd) (link-step! (link-for! cd) now)))`. Note: calling `:step` on a channel that hasn't had a link yet creates the link (via link-for!). So stepping a reachable but not-yet-attached channel creates an empty link and records now. Harmless, returns a map with channel-gone? false, expired nil. Spec says \"nil for channels this peer doesn't reach\" — reachable channels return the map. Creation on step means subsequent attach reuses the same link with :now pre-set — actually beneficial. Fine.\n\n6. Policy validation in `links`: give-up-after added to both select-keys and validation. Good.\n\n7. Portability: no host-specific code. `<=` on numbers — in CLJS, `<=` on ints is fine. Overflow: JS numbers — now + give-up-after could exceed 2^53 for absurd values, irrelevant. Dart ints are 64-bit; fine.\n\n8. Memory leaks: `:pending` is retained after channel-loss! — entries with deadlines remain in the map. `channel-loss!` clears `:outstanding` but not `:pending`. After gone, `retry-pending!` isn't run (drain! early returns), `send-request!`... wait, can a reflection still call send-request! after gone? `refl-cursor` checks `(when-not (:channel-gone? @link) (send-or-count! ...))`. `admit-probe!` checks `(not (:channel-gone? @link))`. So no new pending. Pending is bounded by max-outstanding. Leak is bounded — author already noted as inert. P4.\n\nAlso `:filed`/`:filed-cursors` are retained after loss (spec says \"filed responses are kept and still returned\"). Bounded by max-filed. OK.\n\n9. Now, `link-step!` with nil `now` issue — how severe? The public `:step` contract says `(h channel-descriptor now)`. If a driver passes nil (e.g., dial's own 1-arity path guards, but other drivers may not), `(<= deadline nil)` throws in CLJ (NPE), in CLJS `(<= 10 nil)` — comparison with nil throws \"TypeError: nil cannot be coerced\"? In CLJS, `<=` on nil throws. Only if there's an entry with a deadline; empty link with nil now: filter over empty → fine. And `(swap! link assoc :now nil)` erases now. So: calling `(step cd nil)` on a link that has stamped requests throws. This is a real robustness gap in a public entry. The spec says \"without a stepped now nothing expires\" — one could argue a nil-now step should be a no-op scan. But dial-step!'s 2-arity delegates nil (`([dial] (dial-step! dial nil))`), and the guard `(some? now)` prevents calling link's step with nil. So within this composition it's unreachable. But `links` is public API for other compositions (acceptor-side? acceptor doesn't use links... session-end consumers do). A P3 finding: `link-step!` should guard nil now (skip scan and don't erase) or validate. Also erasing `:now` affects future stamping.\n\nActually wait — is there any place in ws_project that calls `(:step! ch)` with nil? No: it's guarded. But `establish!` is... also guarded. OK, P3.\n\n10. Another check: `send-request!`'s deadline lookup order. `deadline` is computed before we know if the send was accepted. For a fresh request (no pending entry): `(new-deadline link)`. Then if accepted → registered with deadline. If refused-full and descriptor with room → stored in pending with deadline. Spec: \"stamped at the first moment (accepted or kept) that the request exists on the link.\" Good. If refused-full and descriptor and no room → `(swap! refl assoc :probe req)` — waits on reflection, unstamped. Good.\n\nBut edge: `refl-append` refused send → no registration, no deadline — append never becomes pending. Good (\"refused append! didn't cross\").\n\n11. Non-advance of resends: `count-ask!` — verified.\n\n12. Clearing on response: `absorb!` dissocs the outstanding entry. `absorb-named!` also dissocs. Good.\n\n13. Does `link-step!` emit channel-expired before drain!-induced loss? drain! could hit `end` → channel-loss! → gone → skips scan → expired nil, gone true. Event order is fine (end-loss doesn't emit channel-expired; correct per spec — that's a true loss, not an expiry).\n\n14. Test coverage: read the test file to verify claimed tests.\n\n15. Design document changes — read §2.4 Expiry of dao.stream.remote.md and Deferred Liveness of dao.stream.ws.md.\n\nAlso check whether the docstring in `register!` is updated. It is.\n\nAnother potential issue: in `link-step!`, the scan for overdue includes `pending` entries. But after `channel-loss!`, the pending entries remain. If the driver keeps stepping with increasing now, the scan is guarded by channel-gone?, so no re-expiry. Good.\n\nAlso `(concat outstanding pending)` — if the same id somehow appears in both (shouldn't), sort-by key keeps one; non-issue.\n\nType consistency: deadlines are computed as `(+ now give-up-after)`. `now` could be a double (e.g., a driver passes a floating-point ms timestamp) while give-up-after is validated as an integer. `(<= 10.5 10)` is fine. Mixed numeric comparisons are fine on all three hosts. On Dart, int + double = double; fine.\n\nOne subtle CLJS/Dart concern: `sort-by key` on numeric ids — fine.\n\n`some->` inside filter: `(some-> (:deadline e) (<= now))` — threads deadline as first arg to `(<= now)`: `(<= deadline now)`. Correct.\n\nNow, potential issue: `link-step!` records `:now` even when `now` equals an earlier value — fine.\n\nAnother check: `dial-step!` when channel exists but handle was closed earlier (channel already gone from a prior tick). `stepped` returns `{:channel-gone? true :expired nil}` → closes handle again — close! is idempotent. Fine.\n\n`dial-step!`'s `when (some? now) (swap! dial assoc :now now)` — stores now on dial for establish! use. But note: if dial is stepped before the channel is established (typical — does the driver step before attach?), then `dial-attach!` establishes with the last now. Good.\n\nAlso check: in `establish!`, the `now` key is destructured from `@dial` — dial atom has no initial `:now` key (not in dial constructor) — nil until first stepped. Fine.\n\nNow, one deeper question: does `establish!`'s `((:step ls) cd now)` cause a drain on the newly-created link? drain! on the fresh link: reads the ring from the oldest cursor. The ring may already contain traffic? The ring is a channel ring — freshly composed per dial; by establish time, has the projection already been running? The projection is created inside establish! after links. Actually, the ring could contain values if... projection appends to ring; projection is created here. Before establish!, nothing appended (unless the composition did). drain! would just read until blocked. Harmless. Also drain! runs count-ask! for descriptor probes — none yet. OK.\n\nBut wait — subtle point: in dial-step!, the ordering is projection step first, then link step (which drains the ring), then mirror. The link's drain reads responses appended by this tick's projection. Good ordering: responses deposited in this tick can be absorbed before the expiry scan — meaning, a response arriving at the deadline tick prevents a false expiry. Actually the deadline scan happens after drain within link-step!, so an arriving response clears outstanding before the scan. Good design.\n\nPotential false-positive scenario: budget-saturated drain-budget → response sits unread in ring → expiry. Spec explicitly allows false loss due to flooding (D3). Fine.\n\nNow let me read the tests and documents.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Sources read. Now I'll check the tests and the design doc amendments to verify coverage claims.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_49aecc67cf894462931a6ccb","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"deftest|defn (stepped-links|attach-via|step-at|gone|dial-with|toy-cd)","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc","output_mode":"content"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_a7c61206aaad4d31911f24d8","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"deftest|defn (dial-with|toy-cd)","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/test/dao/stream/ws_project_test.cljc","output_mode":"content"}}]}}
