{"type":"event","event":{"type":"tool_completed","toolCallId":"call_e2f2aa58a1964fe19ac69366","toolName":"read_file","result":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 120 lines\nNote: showing lines 340-459. More of the file remains. Use offset=460 to continue.\n340:   elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n341:   is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is dropped.\n342: - `close!`: local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards\n343:   `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n344:   filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n345: \n346: **Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n347: reflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n348: answers `transport-error` with\n349: `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n350: `descriptor` still answers `ok`: a descriptor outlives what it described.\n351: A filed\n352: `no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n353: operation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n354: `:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n355: gone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n356: the channel writer refuses with `full` (establishment, back pressure)\n357: leaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered:\n358: the retry cases above, `blocked` for `next`.\n359: \n360: **Channel loss.** A channel ends when its reader answers `end`: the\n361: projection closed it (3.1), the pair adapter closed it on an `in` gap or\n362: end (3.3), or the medium is gone; or a driver step finds a request past\n363: its deadline (Expiry, below). The link then abandons its outstanding\n364: ids and reports each\n365: abandoned `append!` on the event writer as `append-unknown` (2.5), and\n366: answers later `cursor` and `next` `transport-error` with\n367: `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`, not retryable;\n368: `append!` keeps answering the channel writer's own outcome, `closed` once\n369: the channel is down. Filed answers are still returned. Reattachment is the\n370: caller's policy, by `attach!` on the same descriptor.\n371: \n372: **Expiry.** `links` also gives `:step`, `(step channel-descriptor now)`:\n373: the driver's step of that channel's link at `now`, nil for a channel this\n374: peer does not reach. It records `now` on the link, drains (within the drain\n375: budget), and then, if an outstanding or kept request has `:deadline <=\n376: now`, appends `{:dao.stream.remote/event\n377: :dao.stream.remote/channel-expired :dao.stream.remote/id id\n378: :dao.stream.remote/op op}` for the least such id to the event writer and\n379: loses the channel as above. It answers `{:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone?\n380: bool :dao.stream.remote/expired id-or-nil}`. With\n381: `:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after ms`, a request's deadline is the last\n382: stepped `now` plus `ms`, stamped the first moment the request exists on\n383: the link: its accepted send, or its keeping as a refused attach probe, a\n384: kept probe carrying that deadline into its later send. A probe waiting on\n385: its reflection has none until the link admits it. A resend never moves a\n386: deadline, and an answer clears the request with its deadline. Without\n387: `give-up-after`, or before any step with `now`, nothing is stamped and\n388: nothing expires. `give-up-after` is a liveness statement about the channel,\n389: not the request: on an ordered reliable channel an unanswered request past\n390: it means the peer is not serving. A flood of unrelated values cannot defer\n391: it, because the drain budget bounds each step's reads while `now`\n392: advances; under such a flood the loss may be false, which costs a\n393: reattachment and invalidates nothing. Whatever owns the connection closes\n394: it on loss: `dao.stream.ws-project`'s `dial-step!` given `now` closes its\n395: ws handle.\n396: \n397: **Resolve.** A link also answers `resolve` `name`, for no reflection. It\n398: drains, then returns and forgets a filed answer for the name: `ok` with\n399: the remote descriptor of the identity answered over the link's channel,\n400: `:dao.stream/identity` and `:dao.stream.remote/surface`; or the filed\n401: `not-found` as `transport-error` with\n402: `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, which marks\n403: nothing. A filed `oversize` is returned the same way, with its own name as\n404: the reason. Otherwise it sends the named request if none is outstanding for\n405: the name and answers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true`, as\n406: `cursor` does. A send the channel writer refuses with `full` is retried by\n407: the next `resolve`; any other refusal is returned as the writer's own\n408: outcome, leaves nothing outstanding and is remembered nowhere, so each\n409: `resolve` of that name attempts the send again. A named answer is filed\n410: only when its id is outstanding and it echoes the name asked. An answer\n411: that carries no `:dao.stream/identity` completes no identity request.\n412: After channel loss `resolve` answers `transport-error` with\n413: `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`. On a channel\n414: descriptor this peer holds no channel for, `resolve` answers `not-found`,\n415: as `attach!` does. The caller attaches the descriptor answered with\n416: `attach!`; from there nothing in this section changes.\n417: \n418: **Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n419: operation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n420: (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n421: \n422: **Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote\n423: descriptor's source declares plus `:closable`, learned from the attach\n424: probe's answer (2.1); before that answer it declares\n425: `#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source turns out to\n426: lack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked` is\n427: handle-relative, as the contract's Concurrency section already makes every\n428: outcome: one perspective at one moment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never\n429: answers `cursor-mismatch` or `invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only\n430: relayed, and `append!` never answers the source's `full`, which arrives on\n431: the event writer.\n432: \n433: ### 2.5 Loss and resend\n434: \n435: The reflection reads no clock: the only time a link knows is the `now` a\n436: driver hands its `:step` (2.4, Expiry). A link's\n437: `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n438: means: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\n439: when the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n440: `k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\n441: three operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n442: or a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent:\n443: deduplication and correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an\n444: append whose answer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the\n445: event writer as `{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n446: :dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends\n447: or expires. On UDP (3.2) compose `resend-after` small and `give-up-after`\n448: generous or nil.\n449: There are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no result\n450: retention at the answering peer: the cursor is the sequence number for\n451: reads, and `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n452: \n453: ## 3. Channels\n454: \n455: A channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n456: is ordered and reliable; the link reads the last as composition data for\n457: `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, and nothing else in section 2 changes\n458: per channel.\n459: "}],"deferred":false}}
