{"type":"event","event":{"type":"message_update","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Docs look complete and accurate. Now let me do a final code-level check on the points I flagged:\n\n1. **nil `now` passed to `link-step!`**: `(step cd nil)` — erases `:now`, and if an entry with a stamped deadline exists, `(<= deadline nil)` throws. On CLJS, `(<= 10 nil)` throws TypeError. Severity: unreachable via dial-step! (guarded), but `:step` is public API. P3.\n\n   Also, even without a deadline, `(step cd nil)` clears a previously recorded `:now`, so subsequent sends go without a deadline — surprising behavior. Recommendation: in link-step!, when now is nil, skip recording and scanning (or throw ex-info).\n\n2. **`send-named!` calls `new-deadline` twice** — P4 cosmetic; pure function so no correctness issue. But is it really pure? `new-deadline` derefs an atom; if there are no concurrent swaps between the two calls, same value. Per docstring, driver-only step discipline. P4.\n\n3. **`:pending` is retained even after channel-loss!** — bounded by max-outstanding, but unbounded if max-outstanding is nil. After channel vanishes, pending probes are never retried nor cleared (until reflection close! removes its own entries; reflection that never closes leaks). Existing issue (channel-loss! has not cleared :pending since before S2c — needs confirmation: S2b's channel-loss! also didn't clear pending, so existing). The author flagged this in note 2 and declared it harmless per prior sign-off. P4.\n\n4. **Non-numeric `now` passed to dial-step!**: no validation. Elsewhere in the codebase (make-acceptor's idle-timeout) it validates. Minor; driver-supplied value. P4.\n\n5. **`(swap! link assoc :now now)` inside link-step!** — records now before drain!. So if a new request is sent during drain!, could it be stamped with the new now? drain! only does count-ask! and retry-pending! (kept probes carry their deadline). No new deadline is stamped inside drain! (retry-pending! → send-request! → looks up pending entry → carries existing deadline). Good — the lookup happens first so no re-stamping. But edge case: kept probe without a deadline (kept before any step, i.e., stamped nil because now was nil). Then driver steps with now → retry-pending! → send-request! → pending entry found but `(:deadline kept)` is nil → deadline nil → kept/registered without a deadline → that request never expires. Is this spec-conformant? Spec: deadline is stamped at \"the first moment a request exists on the link\". It existed at a time without a clock, so no deadline — consistent with \"nothing expires before a step with now\"… hmm, but this request exists on the link and survived clock introduction. Literal spec: \"the last stepped now plus give-up-after, stamped at the first moment a request exists on the link\". The first moment was clockless → no stamp. Design doc says \"nothing is stamped and nothing expires before a step with now\" — so probes kept before clock was introduced are never stamped. Consequence: in dial-step-with-now tests, `(dial-step! dial 0)` is executed before attach precisely to ensure the probe has a deadline. In actual operation, dial establishes at the first dial-attach!, and if the driver has never stepped with now, the probe waits indefinitely until the first step… but establish! steps the new link with dial's last now — if nil, no stamp. Subsequent dial-step! calls with now won't stamp existing kept/outstanding entries. Meaning a composition that attaches before ever stepping with now has undying probes, partially defeating the establishment-stall bound. Hmm — is this a real gap?\n\n   Scenario: driver composes dial, calls dial-attach! (probe sent, unstamped because now is nil), then calls dial-step! with now. The probe's outstanding entry has no deadline → never expires → establishment stall unbounded. Spec's intent (ws.md's Deferred liveness) is to bound establishment stalls. The author's establish! fix only covers the case where dial has already been stepped with now. This is a real design-level gap, but one could also say it's per spec: \"the driver supplies now via step; nothing expires without a stepped now\" — but here the driver did step with now, just after the send. Spec says deadlines are stamped at first send… with the \"last stepped now\". If there's no stepped now at first send, no stamp, and no subsequent re-stamp (\"resend never moves deadline\" — and stamping only happens at first existence).\n\n   Options: stamp on deadline-less entry during link-step!? No — spec explicitly states stamping only at first existence, and mob rejected deadline movement on retry. But \"backfilling\" a deadline at the first step with now for an unstamped request is different from \"moving\" a deadline. Not in spec. Severity: P3 — a composition that attaches before its first timed step will never expire its first probe (and requests sent before the first timed step). Worth flagging to architect; either accept as documented behavior, or amend spec to backfill at first timed step. The ws_project dial-step! test avoids this by stepping first. Document this as a P3 finding.\n\n   Actually wait — does this affect the acceptor side too? Acceptor doesn't use links. Consumers (yin) currently use 1-arity. So impact is limited to future S3a drivers. Still deserves P3.\n\n6. **link-step!'s deadline scan includes `pending`** — matches spec (\"outstanding or pending entries\").\n\n7. **Return value `:expired (first overdue)`** — nil when no overdue. Also returns expired id when channel-loss! was already true before this step? Guarded by `when-not channel-gone?` → overdue is nil. Good.\n\n8. **Event ordering test** — channel-expired then append-unknown, matches spec.\n\n9. **Concurrency/step discipline in dial-step!** — mirror effects outside atom updates, tested in dial-mirror-effects-run-once-per-step. The 2-arity still runs mirror only once. Good.\n\n10. **Portability**: `some->`, `concat`, `sort-by key` — all portable. `<=` on numbers also portable. No reader conditionals needed. Catch clauses unchanged. Good.\n\n11. **Overflow/type**: give-up-after is validated as positive integer; `now` is a driver-supplied number. `+` of int+int on Dart/JS is fine. Mixed `(<= deadline now)` also fine.\n\n12. Confirming the `flood-cannot-defer-expiry` test: 60 junk values on `ba` (link's reader). drain-budget 2, step at 5 → 2 reads; step at 10 → 2 more reads, then scan finds probe id 0 with deadline 10 ≤ 10 → expires. Wait — do junk values count as reads? Yes, drain reads and discards. Probe deadline 10 (stamped at step 0 on attach… attach happens after step-at 0, and the probe is stamped at send time when new-deadline reads :now 0 → deadline 10). Good.\n\n13. `a-resend-does-not-move-the-deadline`: resend-after 1; on each `(stream/next r 0)` call: drain! → count-ask! against outstanding descriptor probe… note: probe (id 0) is outstanding, and next request is id 1. count-ask on probe re-sends probe too. No issue. Deadline of id 1 stays at 10. Step at 10 → vanishes. But wait: after `step-at ls t 9`, `stream/next r 0` — the drain inside next doesn't expire (no clock on drain). Then step at 10 expires. Test asserts `(:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? ...)`. Good.\n\n14. Another confirmation — the `resolve` path stamps deadline via send-named! and can expire; covered by `a-request-past-its-deadline...` which asserts resolve answers gone after vanishing. There's no test for expiration of a named resolve request itself (send-named!'s deadline stamp). Minor coverage gap — send-named!'s stamping path is untested (only identity path tested). P4/P3? The cond-> double new-deadline call is inside send-named! — untested. Should be caught if broken (resolve would never expire). Coverage gap: P4 recommendation to add a test where an unanswered resolve expires via :step.\n\n    Hmm, actual severity: the code path exists and appears correct. Lack of test is P4.\n\n15. `a-step-of-an-unreached-channel-is-nil` — checks nil for unreached. Good.\n\n16. `drain-budget-counts-malformed-values-and-a-gap` (S2b follow-up) — exists.\n\n17. `dial-propagates-link-options` — exists, checks give-up-after propagation.\n\n18. Confirming `:step` entry in `links` docstring — documented. `attacher` docstring mentions give-up-after. Good.\n\n19. Confirming `room?` / max-outstanding interaction with pending retention after vanishing — pending remains and counts toward room?; after vanishing, nothing is sent (guards check channel-gone?). OK.\n\n20. `link-step!` returns a map — but what if drain! set channel-gone? via end? Returns `{:channel-gone? true :expired nil}`. dial-step! then closes handle — the ring… end means the reader answered end, i.e., ring is already closed; closing handle is idempotent and correct (dial owns the connection). No issue.\n\n21. Potential issue in `dial-step!`: `stepped` is computed before mirror-step, but handle close happens after mirror-step. Even if channel vanishes, mirror step executes — responds to pending requests on the ring one last time. Harmless.\n\n22. `dial-step!`'s 1-arity: `(dial-step! dial nil)` → no `:now` recording, no link step. But establish! uses dial's `:now` — remains nil. Consistent.\n\nAlso check ws.md's Deferred section rewrite (around line 695) — confirmed, points to remote 2.4 Expiry. Good.\n\nAnother portability check: `sort-by key` over `concat` of two maps — fine in CLJS. `(first overdue)` when overdue is a vector `[id e]` — map-entry is a vector, so it works. In CLJS, map entries are vectors — yes.\n\nAlso `some->` with `(<= now)` — `<=` is a function; `(some-> d (<= now))` → `(<= d now)`. Correct.\n\nConfirm `link-step!` handles non-numeric `now` → throws in filter. Also unguarded. Merge with finding 1 (nil now) → recommend `when (some? now)` guard.\n\nFurther consideration: `link-step!` records `:now` even when channel-gone? — harmless.\n\nVerifying the author's claims about `register!` — now takes deadline parameter; `send-request!` and `refl-append` pass it. Confirmed by reading code.\n\nPotential subtle bug in `send-request!`: deadline is computed via pending lookup — but for a new probe (attach path), `send-request!` is called; on full + descriptor + room → pending entry with fresh deadline. Then retry-pending! → send-request! again → pending lookup → carries deadline. If accepted → register with that deadline, dissoc pending. Correct, no re-stamping.\n\nEdge: what if a probe that was pending (with deadline) is retried, accepted, and then answered? absorb! clears outstanding. Good.\n\nEdge: `admit-probe!` for `:probe` (waiting on reflection, unstamped): send-request! → no pending entry → new-deadline at admission time → stamped with last stepped now. Spec says stamp on admission — matches.\n\nNow, memory-leak review: outstanding cleared on vanishing; pending retained (bounded by max-outstanding if configured; unbounded if nil — but pending entries are only created via descriptor probes with room? check, and room? is unbounded when max-outstanding is nil… how many pending probes could accumulate? One per reflection (each attach probe). Reflections are created by attach — an attach storm creates unbounded reflections and probes. This is an existing S2b issue, not S2c). filed is bounded by max-filed. `:now` is scalar. No new leaks introduced by S2c, with the exception of pending retention after vanishing, but that's existing channel-loss! behavior.\n\nArithmetic: `(+ now give-up-after)` — CLJS numbers are doubles; large ms epochs (~1.7e12) + small ints are exact below 2^53. Dart int is 64-bit. Fine.\n\nConcurrency: all swap! are on driver thread per discipline; new-deadline derefs without swap — fine.\n\nOK let me assemble findings:\n\n- P3-1: `link-step!` given a nil (or non-numeric) `now` erases the link's `:now` and, if a stamped deadline exists, throws on comparison. The public `:step` entry does not guard. Unreachable via ws-project (guarded), but links is public API. Recommendation: in link-step!, `when (some? now)` guard around recording and scanning (or validate number and throw composition error).\n\n- P3-2: Requests sent before the first timed step never get a deadline — including dial compositions where `dial-attach!` runs before any `(dial-step! dial now)`. `establish!`'s step covers only when dial was previously stepped with now. Literal spec behavior (\"nothing is stamped before a step with now\"), but leaves establishment stall unbounded in an attach-first ordering — precisely the gap Expiry was meant to close. Recommendation: either document that drivers must step with now before attach, or (with architect approval) backfill deadlines at the first timed step for entries that lack them; a one-line note in docs may suffice.\n\n- P4-1: `send-named!` computes `(new-deadline link)` twice inside cond->. Pure function, but should be let-bound. Also, no test exercises the resolve request deadline/expiration path.\n\n- P4-2: `channel-loss!` leaves `:pending` entries (with deadlines) behind; harmless per sign-off, but retains reflection references until each reflection closes. Consider dissoc'ing pending in channel-loss! for hygiene (author notes this was intentionally skipped).\n\n- P4-3: `dial-step!`'s 2-arity doesn't validate that `now` is a number; a non-numeric now flows to link-step! and throws deep in comparison. (Could fold into P3-1.)\n\nPositive verifications:\n- Policy validation for give-up-after added to links (0, -1, 1.5 tested).\n- Stamping rules: stamp on accept, carry over kept probe (lookup before new-deadline — never re-stamped), waiting probes unstamped until admission, count-ask! never touches deadline, absorb! clears entry+deadline.\n- link-step!: drain first (so an answer arriving in the deadline tick clears before scan), scan skipped on channel-gone?, minimum id over outstanding+pending, event shape exact, channel-loss! after emit, return shape exact.\n- dial-step! 2-arity: projection → link step → mirror → close handle on gone; 1-arity delegates with nil; establish! steps new link with last now.\n- Portability: no host-specific code; `.-state` access in tests is pre-existing; all seq/comparison functions clj/cljs/cljd-safe; no overflow concerns.\n- Test coverage: all 7 required remote tests + 3 ws tests present, plus S2b follow-ups.\n\nVerdict: ACCEPT with non-blocking findings (P3s are robustness/documentation-level; core spec is met). Hmm — P3-2: is this within the spec's literal? Spec §1.3: \"if `:now` has not yet been recorded (driver has never stepped with now)… no deadline is stamped and nothing expires: clockless composition unchanged.\" A composition that attaches then steps with now is not clockless — it becomes clocked after the first step. The first probe remains unstamped forever. The mob's spec states deadline = \"ms since first accepted or retained send of request\" — measuring from first send requires knowing now at first send. Architect spec design rule 5: \"deadline is driver-supplied now, fixed at first send\". If now unavailable at first send, spec is silent. The author's establish! fix shows they recognized this matters in dial path. Ordering where attach precedes step: in ws_project, establish! uses dial's saved :now, so driver that steps before attach (even once) is covered. Driver that never steps before attach → probe unstamped → but subsequent dial-step! with now would… probe eventually answered or ws closed. Only pathological if peer blackholes. Given spec silence, flag as P3 and recommend documentation clarification or backfill, not blocking.\n\nVerdict: ACCEPT (with P3 follow-ups recorded for architect / S3a).\n\nNow, plan mode constraint: cannot write to collab/. Write the full findings doc to ~/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2c-review-qwen3.8-max.md and tell user we need to exit plan mode (or grant permission) to copy to the target collab path. Actually the plan file is the only permitted write. The review report is a deliverable, not a plan, but writing it there is the only option. Write the report content into the plan file, plus a one-line \"implementation step\": copy this file to collab/1791359934597-stream-s2c-review.qwen3.8-max.findings.md.\n\nBefore writing, let me double-check a few code details I want to state precisely:\n\n- In `link-step!`, when `now` is nil and no deadlines: returns map with channel-gone? and expired nil — but erases :now. Confirmed by reading code.\n\n- Verify `the-one-arity-dial-step-never-expires` asserts resolve answers retry — yes.\n\n- Verify `dial-step-with-now-closes-the-handle-on-expiry`: steps at 0 first, then attach — establish! steps link at 0 → probe stamped with deadline 10. Step at 9 → append to sock still ok (handle open). Step at 10 → expires → handle closed → append answers closed. Then :ws/closed emitted, step at 11 → projection closed, ring closed, resolve answers channel-gone.\n\nWait, one nit in that test: after handle close, `dial-resolve!` — resolve-name first drains!; ring is closed at step 11 (drain reads end → channel-loss! → gone… already gone). Then `(:channel-gone? @link)` → transformed channel-gone. Good.\n\nAlso confirming: `dial-step!` at 10 closes handle; then at 11 the projection step reads :ws/closed → closes ring → mirror-step on closed ring… mirror reads ring at its cursor; closed ring answers end → mirror returns cursor. Fine.\n\nAlso, checking whether ws handle's `stream/close!` is idempotent — sock is `(ring 8)` test fixture; ring's close! is idempotent. Fine.\n\nOne more check on `link-step!` — `(sort-by key)` over concat of outstanding/pending: both keyed by integer id. OK.\n\nAnd `emit!` before `channel-loss!`: channel-expired event precedes append-unknown events. Test verifies order. Spec says fire channel-expired then call channel-loss!. Matches.\n\nNow, write the plan/report file. Path: ~/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2c-review-qwen3.8-max.md. Wait — plan directory is ~/.commandcode/plans/. Home is /Users/sto. Use absolute path /Users/sto/.commandc