"name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc","offset":2034}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_1ff1632bea40415dbc418ef1","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/test/dao/stream/ws_project_test.cljc","offset":700}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791360181362,"messageId":"30f5acac-22fa-4e97-870e-9e6cf690b625"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_a930696ed02a404780d44aa0","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 218 lines\n2034: (deftest a-prefetch-never-evicts-its-own-answer\n2035:   (let [{:keys [t peer r link]}\n2036:         (listed-reflection [:a :b :c]\n2037:                            {:dao.stream.remote/budget 2\n2038:                             :dao.stream.remote/max-filed 1})]\n2039:     (is (= :dao.stream/blocked (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/next r 0))))\n2040:     (is (= 1 (:dao.stream.remote/budget\n2041:                (first (op-requests (:ab t) :dao.stream/next))))\n2042:         \"the stamped budget is no more than the link can retain\")\n2043:     (doseq [[c v] [[0 :a] [1 :b] [2 :c]]]\n2044:       (when (pos? c)\n2045:         (is (= :dao.stream/blocked (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/next r c)))\n2046:             \"nothing prefetched past the retained capacity\"))\n2047:       (serve! peer)\n2048:       (is (= v (:dao.stream/value (stream/next r c))) (str \"reads \" v))\n2049:       (is (<= (+ (count (:filed @link)) (count (:filed-cursors @link))) 1)))\n2050:     (serve! peer)\n2051:     (is (= :dao.stream/blocked (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/next r 3)))\n2052:         \"the source's own blocked at its end\")))\n2053: \n2054: \n2055: ;; =============================================================================\n2056: ;; Request liveness: deadlines over a driver-stepped now (S2c)\n2057: ;; =============================================================================\n2058: \n2059: (defn- stepped-links\n2060:   \"Links over the toy's channel whose writer is `writer` (the toy's own\n2061:    ab buffer when nil), with event writer `events` and policy `opts`.\"\n2062:   ([t events opts] (stepped-links t events opts nil))\n2063:   ([t events opts writer]\n2064:    (remote/links (merge {:dao.stream.remote/channels\n2065:                          {(:channel t) {:reader (:ba t)\n2066:                                         :writer (or writer (:ab t))}}\n2067:                          :dao.stream.remote/events events}\n2068:                         opts))))\n2069: \n2070: \n2071: (defn- attach-via\n2072:   [ls t identity]\n2073:   (:dao.stream/handle\n2074:     ((:attach ls) {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote\n2075:                    :dao.stream/identity identity\n2076:                    :dao.stream/channel (:channel t)})))\n2077: \n2078: \n2079: (defn- step-at\n2080:   [ls t now]\n2081:   ((:step ls) (:channel t) now))\n2082: \n2083: \n2084: (def ^:private gone\n2085:   {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n2086:    :dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone})\n2087: \n2088: \n2089: (deftest a-request-past-its-deadline-is-channel-loss\n2090:   (let [t (toy)\n2091:         ev (ring 16)\n2092:         ls (stepped-links t ev {:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after 10})\n2093:         _ (step-at ls t 0)\n2094:         r (attach-via ls t \"n\")\n2095:         link (link-state r)]\n2096:     (stream/next r 0)\n2097:     (let [append-id (:dao.stream.remote/id\n2098:                       (do (stream/append! r :v)\n2099:                           (last (op-requests (:ab t) :dao.stream/append!))))]\n2100:       (is (every? #(= 10 (:deadline %)) (vals (:outstanding @link)))\n2101:           \"probe, next and append! each carry now 0 plus 10\")\n2102:       (is (= {:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? false\n2103:               :dao.stream.remote/expired nil}\n2104:              (step-at ls t 9))\n2105:           \"before the deadline nothing changes\")\n2106:       (is (= {:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? true\n2107:               :dao.stream.remote/expired 0}\n2108:              (step-at ls t 10))\n2109:           \"the least overdue id, the probe\")\n2110:       (is (= [{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/channel-expired\n2111:                :dao.stream.remote/id 0\n2112:                :dao.stream.remote/op :dao.stream/descriptor}\n2113:               {:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n2114:                :dao.stream.remote/id append-id}]\n2115:              (values ev)))\n2116:       (is (:channel-gone? @link))\n2117:       (is (= {} (:outstanding @link)))\n2118:       (is (= gone (stream/next r 0)) \"not retryable\")\n2119:       (is (= gone (stream/cursor r :dao.stream/oldest)))\n2120:       (is (= gone ((:resolve ls) (:channel t) \"nm\")))\n2121:       (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/descriptor r)))\n2122:           \"a name outlives what it named\"))))\n2123: \n2124: \n2125: (deftest a-resend-does-not-move-the-deadline\n2126:   (let [t (toy)\n2127:         ls (stepped-links t nil {:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after 10\n2128:                                  :dao.stream.remote/resend-after 1})\n2129:         _ (step-at ls t 0)\n2130:         r (attach-via ls t \"n\")]\n2131:     (stream/next r 0)\n2132:     (doseq [now [3 6 9]]\n2133:       (step-at ls t now)\n2134:       (stream/next r 0))\n2135:     (is (< 1 (count (op-requests (:ab t) :dao.stream/next)))\n2136:         \"the next request was re-sent\")\n2137:     (is (= 10 (:deadline (get (:outstanding @(link-state r)) 1))))\n2138:     (is (:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? (step-at ls t 10))\n2139:         \"expiry stays at the first send plus give-up-after\")))\n2140: \n2141: \n2142: (deftest an-answer-before-the-deadline-clears-it\n2143:   (let [t (toy)\n2144:         peer (served-peer {\"n\" (entry (list-stream \"n\" [:a]) #{:reader})} t)\n2145:         ls (stepped-links t nil {:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after 10})\n2146:         _ (step-at ls t 0)\n2147:         r (attach-via ls t \"n\")]\n2148:     (serve! peer)\n2149:     (step-at ls t 5)\n2150:     (stream/next r 0)\n2151:     (serve! peer)\n2152:     (step-at ls t 12)\n2153:     (is (= {} (:outstanding @(link-state r))) \"both answered in time\")\n2154:     (is (= {:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? false\n2155:             :dao.stream.remote/expired nil}\n2156:            (step-at ls t 20)))\n2157:     (is (= :a (:dao.stream/value (stream/next r 0))))))\n2158: \n2159: \n2160: (deftest a-kept-probe-has-a-deadline\n2161:   (let [t (toy)\n2162:         attempts (atom 0)\n2163:         ls (stepped-links t nil {:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after 10}\n2164:                           (counting-writer attempts :dao.stream/full))\n2165:         _ (step-at ls t 0)\n2166:         r (attach-via ls t \"n\")\n2167:         link (link-state r)]\n2168:     (is (= 10 (:deadline (get (:pending @link) 0))) \"kept, with a deadline\")\n2169:     (step-at ls t 5)\n2170:     (is (= 10 (:deadline (get (:pending @link) 0)))\n2171:         \"a refused retry keeps the first deadline\")\n2172:     (is (= {:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? true\n2173:             :dao.stream.remote/expired 0}\n2174:            (step-at ls t 10))\n2175:         \"a writer that only answers full is bounded\")))\n2176: \n2177: \n2178: (deftest without-a-stepped-now-nothing-expires\n2179:   (let [t (toy)\n2180:         ls (stepped-links t nil {:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after 10\n2181:                                  :dao.stream.remote/resend-after 1})\n2182:         r (attach-via ls t \"n\")\n2183:         link (link-state r)]\n2184:     (dotimes [_ 5]\n2185:       (is (= :dao.stream/blocked (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/next r 0)))))\n2186:     (is (not-any? :deadline (vals (:outstanding @link)))\n2187:         \"nothing stamped without a now\")\n2188:     (is (not (:channel-gone? @link)))\n2189:     (is (< 1 (count (op-requests (:ab t) :dao.stream/next)))\n2190:         \"the reflection keeps retrying\")))\n2191: \n2192: \n2193: (deftest flood-cannot-defer-expiry\n2194:   (let [t (toy)\n2195:         ls (stepped-links t nil {:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after 10\n2196:                                  :dao.stream.remote/drain-budget 2})\n2197:         _ (step-at ls t 0)\n2198:         r (attach-via ls t \"n\")\n2199:         link (link-state r)]\n2200:     (dotimes [i 60]\n2201:       (stream/append! (:ba t) {:unrelated i}))\n2202:     (step-at ls t 5)\n2203:     (is (not (:channel-gone? @link)))\n2204:     (is (= {:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? true\n2205:             :dao.stream.remote/expired 0}\n2206:            (step-at ls t 10))\n2207:         \"two reads per step, and the deadline still passes\")))\n2208: \n2209: \n2210: (deftest an-invalid-give-up-after-is-a-composition-error\n2211:   (let [t (toy)]\n2212:     (doseq [bad [0 -1 1.5]]\n2213:       (is (thrown? #?(:clj Exception :cljs js/Error :cljd Object)\n2214:             (stepped-links t nil {:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after bad}))\n2215:           (pr-str bad)))))\n2216: \n2217: \n2218: (deftest a-step-of-an-unreached-channel-is-nil\n2219:   (let [t (toy)\n2220:         ls (stepped-links t nil {})]\n2221:     (is (nil? ((:step ls) {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream.test/channel\n2222:                            :dao.stream/identity \"elsewhere\"}\n2223:                           0)))))\n2224: \n2225: \n2226: (deftest drain-budget-counts-malformed-values-and-a-gap\n2227:   (let [ab (ring 64)\n2228:         ba (ring 2)\n2229:         cd {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream.test/channel\n2230:             :dao.stream/identity \"toy-channel\"}\n2231:         ls (remote/links {:dao.stream.remote/channels\n2232:                           {cd {:reader ba :writer ab}}\n2233:                           :dao.stream.remote/drain-budget 2})\n2234:         r (:dao.stream/handle\n2235:             ((:attach ls) {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote\n2236:                            :dao.stream/identity \"n\"\n2237:                            :dao.stream/channel cd}))\n2238:         confirmed? #(some? (:surface @(.-state r)))]\n2239:     (doseq [v [:junk-1 :junk-2 {:not :an-answer}]]\n2240:       (stream/append! ba v))\n2241:     (stream/descriptor r)\n2242:     (is (not (confirmed?)) \"the first drain met the gap\")\n2243:     (stream/descriptor r)\n2244:     (is (not (confirmed?)) \"the second read two malformed values\")\n2245:     (stream/append! ba {:dao.stream.remote/id 0\n2246:                         :dao.stream/identity \"n\"\n2247:                         :dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n2248:                         :dao.stream/descriptor {}\n2249:                         :dao.stream.remote/surface #{:reader}})\n2250:     (stream/descriptor r)\n2251:     (is (confirmed?) \"the probe's answer, after the junk and the gap\")))"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_1ff1632bea40415dbc418ef1","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 134 lines\n700:                 :ring channel\n701:                 :table {\"str-1\" {:handle served :surface #{:reader}}}\n702:                 :step-budget 2\n703:                 :mirror-budget 1\n704:                 :chase-budget 2})]\n705:     (project/dial-attach! dial (remote-descriptor \"other\"))\n706:     (let [sock (:handle (project/channel dial))]\n707:       (deposit! medium (payload \"sock-1\" next-req))\n708:       (deposit! medium (payload \"sock-1\" (descriptor-req 2)))\n709:       (deposit! medium (payload \"sock-1\" (descriptor-req 3)))\n710:       (project/dial-step! dial)\n711:       (is (= 2 (count (filter #(contains? % :dao.stream.remote/op)\n712:                               (values channel))))\n713:           \":step-budget 2 projects two of the three requests\")\n714:       (is (= [1] (mapv :dao.stream.remote/id (answers sock)))\n715:           \":mirror-budget 1 answers one\")\n716:       (is (= 1 (count (:dao.stream.remote/more (first (answers sock)))))\n717:           \":chase-budget 2 clamps the peer's budget of 5\")\n718:       (project/dial-step! dial)\n719:       (project/dial-step! dial)\n720:       (is (= [1 2 3] (mapv :dao.stream.remote/id (answers sock)))\n721:           \"the rest follow one per tick\"))))\n722: \n723: \n724: (deftest dial-mirror-effects-run-once-per-step\n725:   ;; The served writer replaces the dial's mirror cursor with an equal\n726:   ;; but distinct value while its append runs: a mirror step run inside\n727:   ;; swap! would then retry and apply the append a second time.\n728:   (let [{:keys [medium channel]} (composed)\n729:         dial-ref (atom nil)\n730:         applied (atom [])\n731:         target (reify stream/IDaoStreamWriter\n732:                  (append!\n733:                    [_ v]\n734:                    (when (= 1 (count (swap! applied conj v)))\n735:                      (swap! (:mirror-cursor @@dial-ref) #(into {} %)))\n736:                    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}))\n737:         dial (project/dial\n738:                {:attach! (fn [_]\n739:                            {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n740:                             :dao.stream/handle (ring 8)\n741:                             :dao.stream/attachment \"sock-1\"})\n742:                 :traffic {:dao.stream/handle medium\n743:                           :dao.stream/surface #{:writer}}\n744:                 :cursor (newest-cursor medium)\n745:                 :ring channel\n746:                 :table {\"w\" {:handle target :surface #{:writer}}}})]\n747:     (reset! dial-ref dial)\n748:     (project/dial-attach! dial (remote-descriptor \"other\"))\n749:     (deposit! medium (payload \"sock-1\" {:dao.stream/identity \"w\"\n750:                                         :dao.stream.remote/op\n751:                                         :dao.stream/append!\n752:                                         :dao.stream.remote/args [:v]\n753:                                         :dao.stream.remote/id 1}))\n754:     (project/dial-step! dial)\n755:     (is (= [:v] @applied) \"the source append ran exactly once\")\n756:     (is (= [1] (mapv :dao.stream.remote/id\n757:                      (answers (:handle (project/channel dial)))))\n758:         \"and was answered once\")))\n759: \n760: \n761: (defn- dial-with\n762:   \"A dial like `dialed`, its fake attacher handing out `sock`, with\n763:    `opts` merged into its composition.\"\n764:   [medium channel-ring sock opts]\n765:   (project/dial\n766:     (merge {:attach! (fn [_]\n767:                        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n768:                         :dao.stream/handle sock\n769:                         :dao.stream/attachment \"sock-1\"})\n770:             :traffic {:dao.stream/handle medium\n771:                       :dao.stream/surface #{:writer}}\n772:             :cursor (newest-cursor medium)\n773:             :ring channel-ring\n774:             :table {}}\n775:            opts)))\n776: \n777: \n778: (def ^:private toy-cd\n779:   (:dao.stream/channel (remote-descriptor \"any\")))\n780: \n781: \n782: (deftest dial-step-with-now-closes-the-handle-on-expiry\n783:   (let [{:keys [medium channel]} (composed)\n784:         sock (ring 8)\n785:         dial (dial-with medium channel sock\n786:                         {:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after 10})]\n787:     (project/dial-step! dial 0)\n788:     (project/dial-attach! dial (remote-descriptor \"str-1\"))\n789:     (project/dial-step! dial 9)\n790:     (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! sock :probe)))\n791:         \"before the deadline the handle stays open\")\n792:     (project/dial-step! dial 10)\n793:     (is (= :dao.stream/closed (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! sock :x)))\n794:         \"the unanswered probe expired: the dial closed the ws handle\")\n795:     (deposit! medium (event \"sock-1\" :ws/closed))\n796:     (project/dial-step! dial 11)\n797:     (is (project/closed? (:project (project/channel dial)))\n798:         \"the host's :ws/closed closed the projection\")\n799:     (is (= :dao.stream/closed (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! channel :x)))\n800:         \"and the ring\")\n801:     (is (= {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n802:             :dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone}\n803:            (project/dial-resolve! dial toy-cd \"nm\")))))\n804: \n805: \n806: (deftest the-one-arity-dial-step-never-expires\n807:   (let [{:keys [medium channel]} (composed)\n808:         sock (ring 64)\n809:         dial (dial-with medium channel sock\n810:                         {:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after 10})]\n811:     (project/dial-attach! dial (remote-descriptor \"str-1\"))\n812:     (dotimes [_ 5] (project/dial-step! dial))\n813:     (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! sock :x)))\n814:         \"the handle stays open\")\n815:     (is (= {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n816:             :dao.stream/retry? true}\n817:            (project/dial-resolve! dial toy-cd \"nm\")))))\n818: \n819: \n820: (deftest dial-propagates-link-options\n821:   (let [{:keys [medium channel]} (composed)\n822:         dial (dial-with medium channel (ring 8)\n823:                         {:dao.stream.remote/drain-budget 3\n824:                          :dao.stream.remote/max-outstanding 4\n825:                          :dao.stream.remote/max-filed 5\n826:                          :dao.stream.remote/give-up-after 6})\n827:         r (:dao.stream/handle\n828:             (project/dial-attach! dial (remote-descriptor \"str-1\")))]\n829:     (is (= {:drain-budget 3 :max-outstanding 4 :max-filed 5\n830:             :give-up-after 6}\n831:            (select-keys @(:link @(.-state r))\n832:                         [:drain-budget :max-outstanding :max-filed\n833:                          :give-up-after])))))"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"f4304411-a510-48f8-8aaf-091bb8c18123"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"the tests appear to match the spec. Note on the test `a-request-past-its-deadline-is-channel-loss`: after expiry, resolve responds with `gone` — resolve-name checks `(:channel-gone? @link)` → the transformed channel-gone. Good.\n\nOne thing to verify: `link-state` — presumably the test helper reads `.-state` of the handle, then `:link`. Also `.-state` is a field access on a deftype — is it portable across clj/cljs/cljd? deftype fields are accessible via `.-state` in CLJS, and in CLJ a deftype's fields are… in Clojure JVM, `.-state` on a deftype instance — are deftype fields accessible via direct field access `.-state` in clj too? Actually, in Clojure, deftype fields can be accessed with `(.field obj)`, and `.-state` compiles to the same thing in clj too. Yes, `.-field` works in Clojure for deftype fields. Also in cljd (Dart). This is something that already exists in the test file (used earlier at line 2238). No issue.\n\nNext, checking the design document. Read the Expiry paragraph of §2.4 in dao.stream.remote.md and Deferred Liveness in ws.md. Let's grep.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Tests look thorough. Now let me verify the design doc amendments.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_e5e6c8b88a7c49f58116e173","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"Expiry|give-up-after|channel-expired|liveness","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md","output_mode":"content","-n":true}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_5549e0c9d965473eba372afe","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"Expiry|give-up-after|liveness|Liveness","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md","output_mode":"content","-n":true}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791360197107,"messageId":"d82814a8-ea8c-452b-b350-782f7bbd4d6b"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_e5e6c8b88a7c49f58116e173","content":[{"type":"text","text":"docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:363:its deadline (Expiry, below). The link then abandons its outstanding\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:372:**Expiry.** `links` also gives `:step`, `(step channel-descriptor now)`:\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:377::dao.stream.remote/channel-expired :dao.stream.remote/id id\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:381:`:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after ms`, a request's deadline is the last\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:387:`give-up-after`, or before any step with `now`, nothing is stamped and\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:388:nothing expires. `give-up-after` is a liveness statement about the channel,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:436:driver hands its `:step` (2.4, Expiry). A link's\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:447:or expires. On UDP (3.2) compose `resend-after` small and `give-up-after`\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:502:- **Request liveness (`give-up-after`)**: `:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after`,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:505:  `:step` (2.4, Expiry); past it the channel is lost. A dialing composition\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:767:**Expiry versus channel loss.** Channel loss is the absence of answers or\ndocs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:770:its lease intact. Expiry is a present answer, the `not-found` error, on a"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_5549e0c9d965473eba372afe","content":[{"type":"text","text":"docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:695:- Liveness: for a connection used as a `dao.stream.remote.md` channel,\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:696:  request liveness is decided there (2.4, Expiry): a request unanswered\ndocs/design/dao.stream.ws.md:697:  past `:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after`, against the `now` a driver hands"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"4487733a-a0a2-4993-b93d-c0f0e0f6cad8"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me read and verify the entire Expiry paragraph (approximately lines 355-450) of dao.stream.remote.md.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_e2f2aa58a1964fe19ac69366","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md","offset":340,"limit":120}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791360207026,"messageId":"59d1e82a-708c-4dda-8092-9f5c4d25cca8"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_e2f2aa58a1964fe19ac69366","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 120 lines\nNote: showing lines 340-459. More of the file remains. Use offset=460 to continue.\n340:   elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n341:   is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is dropped.\n342: - `close!`: local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards\n343:   `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n344:   filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n345: \n346: **Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n347: reflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n348: answers `transport-error` with\n349: `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n350: `descriptor` still answers `ok`: a descriptor outlives what it described.\n351: A filed\n352: `no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n353: operation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n354: `:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n355: gone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n356: the channel writer refuses with `full` (establishment, back pressure)\n357: leaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered:\n358: the retry cases above, `blocked` for `next`.\n359: \n360: **Channel loss.** A channel ends when its reader answers `end`: the\n361: projection closed it (3.1), the pair adapter closed it on an `in` gap or\n362: end (3.3), or the medium is gone; or a driver step finds a request past\n363: its deadline (Expiry, below). The link then abandons its outstanding\n364: ids and reports each\n365: abandoned `append!` on the event writer as `append-unknown` (2.5), and\n366: answers later `cursor` and `next` `transport-error` with\n367: `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`, not retryable;\n368: `append!` keeps answering the channel writer's own outcome, `closed` once\n369: the channel is down. Filed answers are still returned. Reattachment is the\n370: caller's policy, by `attach!` on the same descriptor.\n371: \n372: **Expiry.** `links` also gives `:step`, `(step channel-descriptor now)`:\n373: the driver's step of that channel's link at `now`, nil for a channel this\n374: peer does not reach. It records `now` on the link, drains (within the drain\n375: budget), and then, if an outstanding or kept request has `:deadline <=\n376: now`, appends `{:dao.stream.remote/event\n377: :dao.stream.remote/channel-expired :dao.stream.remote/id id\n378: :dao.stream.remote/op op}` for the least such id to the event writer and\n379: loses the channel as above. It answers `{:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone?\n380: bool :dao.stream.remote/expired id-or-nil}`. With\n381: `:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after ms`, a request's deadline is the last\n382: stepped `now` plus `ms`, stamped the first moment the request exists on\n383: the link: its accepted send, or its keeping as a refused attach probe, a\n384: kept probe carrying that deadline into its later send. A probe waiting on\n385: its reflection has none until the link admits it. A resend never moves a\n386: deadline, and an answer clears the request with its deadline. Without\n387: `give-up-after`, or before any step with `now`, nothing is stamped and\n388: nothing expires. `give-up-after` is a liveness statement about the channel,\n389: not the request: on an ordered reliable channel an unanswered request past\n390: it means the peer is not serving. A flood of unrelated values cannot defer\n391: it, because the drain budget bounds each step's reads while `now`\n392: advances; under such a flood the loss may be false, which costs a\n393: reattachment and invalidates nothing. Whatever owns the connection closes\n394: it on loss: `dao.stream.ws-project`'s `dial-step!` given `now` closes its\n395: ws handle.\n396: \n397: **Resolve.** A link also answers `resolve` `name`, for no reflection. It\n398: drains, then returns and forgets a filed answer for the name: `ok` with\n399: the remote descriptor of the identity answered over the link's channel,\n400: `:dao.stream/identity` and `:dao.stream.remote/surface`; or the filed\n401: `not-found` as `transport-error` with\n402: `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, which marks\n403: nothing. A filed `oversize` is returned the same way, with its own name as\n404: the reason. Otherwise it sends the named request if none is outstanding for\n405: the name and answers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true`, as\n406: `cursor` does. A send the channel writer refuses with `full` is retried by\n407: the next `resolve`; any other refusal is returned as the writer's own\n408: outcome, leaves nothing outstanding and is remembered nowhere, so each\n409: `resolve` of that name attempts the send again. A named answer is filed\n410: only when its id is outstanding and it echoes the name asked. An answer\n411: that carries no `:dao.stream/identity` completes no identity request.\n412: After channel loss `resolve` answers `transport-error` with\n413: `:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`. On a channel\n414: descriptor this peer holds no channel for, `resolve` answers `not-found`,\n415: as `attach!` does. The caller attaches the descriptor answered with\n416: `attach!`; from there nothing in this section changes.\n417: \n418: **Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n419: operation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n420: (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n421: \n422: **Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote\n423: descriptor's source declares plus `:closable`, learned from the attach\n424: probe's answer (2.1); before that answer it declares\n425: `#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source turns out to\n426: lack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked` is\n427: handle-relative, as the contract's Concurrency section already makes every\n428: outcome: one perspective at one moment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never\n429: answers `cursor-mismatch` or `invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only\n430: relayed, and `append!` never answers the source's `full`, which arrives on\n431: the event writer.\n432: \n433: ### 2.5 Loss and resend\n434: \n435: The reflection reads no clock: the only time a link knows is the `now` a\n436: driver hands its `:step` (2.4, Expiry). A link's\n437: `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n438: means: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\n439: when the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n440: `k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\n441: three operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n442: or a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent:\n443: deduplication and correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an\n444: append whose answer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the\n445: event writer as `{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n446: :dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends\n447: or expires. On UDP (3.2) compose `resend-after` small and `give-up-after`\n448: generous or nil.\n449: There are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no result\n450: retention at the answering peer: the cursor is the sequence number for\n451: reads, and `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n452: \n453: ## 3. Channels\n454: \n455: A channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n456: is ordered and reliable; the link reads the last as composition data for\n457: `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, and nothing else in section 2 changes\n458: per channel.\n459: "}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"4a8ff369-0d3b-45f5-a61d-d1d1b8b220ef"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Docs look complete and accurate. Now let me do a final code-level check on the points I flagged:\n\n1. **nil `now` passed to `link-step!`**: `(step cd nil)` — erases `:now`, and if an entry with a stamped deadline exists, `(<= deadline nil)` throws. On CLJS, `(<= 10 nil)` throws TypeError. Severity: unreachable via dial-step! (guarded), but `:step` is public API. P3.\n\n   Also, even without a deadline, `(step cd nil)` clears a previously recorded `:now`, so subsequent sends go without a deadline — surprising behavior. Recommendation: in link-step!, when now is nil, skip recording and scanning (or throw ex-info).\n\n2. **`send-named!` calls `new-deadline` twice** — P4 cosmetic; pure function so no correctness issue. But is it really pure? `new-deadline` derefs an atom; if there are no concurrent swaps between the two calls, same value. Per docstring, driver-only step discipline. P4.\n\n3. **`:pending` is retained even after channel-loss!** — bounded by max-outstanding, but unbounded if max-outstanding is nil. After channel vanishes, pending probes are never retried nor cleared (until reflection close! removes its own entries; reflection that never closes leaks). Existing issue (channel-loss! has not cleared :pending since before S2c — needs confirmation: S2b's channel-loss! also didn't clear pending, so existing). The author flagged this in note 2 and declared it harmless per prior sign-off. P4.\n\n4. **Non-numeric `now` passed to dial-step!**: no validation. Elsewhere in the codebase (make-acceptor's idle-timeout) it validates. Minor; driver-supplied value. P4.\n\n5. **`(swap! link assoc :now now)` inside link-step!** — records now before drain!. So if a new request is sent during drain!, could it be stamped with the new now? drain! only does count-ask! and retry-pending! (kept probes carry their deadline). No new deadline is stamped inside drain! (retry-pending! → send-request! → looks up pending entry → carries existing deadline). Good — the lookup happens first so no re-stamping. But edge case: kept probe without a deadline (kept before any step, i.e., stamped nil because now was nil). Then driver steps with now → retry-pending! → send-request! → pending entry found but `(:deadline kept)` is nil → deadline nil → kept/registered without a deadline → that request never expires. Is this spec-conformant? Spec: deadline is stamped at \"the first moment a request exists on the link\". It existed at a time without a clock, so no deadline — consistent with \"nothing expires before a step with now\"… hmm, but this request exists on the link and survived clock introduction. Literal spec: \"the last stepped now plus give-up-after, stamped at the first moment a request exists on the link\". The first moment was clockless → no stamp. Design doc says \"nothing is stamped and nothing expires before a step with now\" — so probes kept before clock was introduced are never stamped. Consequence: in dial-step-with-now tests, `(dial-step! dial 0)` is executed before attach precisely to ensure the probe has a deadline. In actual operation, dial establishes at the first dial-attach!, and if the driver has never stepped with now, the probe waits indefinitely until the first step… but establish! steps the new link with dial's last now — if nil, no stamp. Subsequent dial-step! calls with now won't stamp existing kept/outstanding entries. Meaning a composition that attaches before ever stepping with now has undying probes, partially defeating the establishment-stall bound. Hmm — is this a real gap?\n\n   Scenario: driver composes dial, calls dial-attach! (probe sent, unstamped because now is nil), then calls dial-step! with now. The probe's outstanding entry has no deadline → never expires → establishment stall unbounded. Spec's intent (ws.md's Deferred liveness) is to bound establishment stalls. The author's establish! fix only covers the case where dial has already been stepped with now. This is a real design-level gap, but one could also say it's per spec: \"the driver supplies now via step; nothing expires without a stepped now\" — but here the driver did step with now, just after the send. Spec says deadlines are stamped at first send… with the \"last stepped now\". If there's no stepped now at first send, no stamp, and no subsequent re-stamp (\"resend never moves deadline\" — and stamping only happens at first existence).\n\n   Options: stamp on deadline-less entry during link-step!? No — spec explicitly states stamping only at first existence, and mob rejected deadline movement on retry. But \"backfilling\" a deadline at the first step with now for an unstamped request is different from \"moving\" a deadline. Not in spec. Severity: P3 — a composition that attaches before its first timed step will never expire its first probe (and requests sent before the first timed step). Worth flagging to architect; either accept as documented behavior, or amend spec to backfill at first timed step. The ws_project dial-step! test avoids this by stepping first. Document this as a P3 finding.\n\n   Actually wait — does this affect the acceptor side too? Acceptor doesn't use links. Consumers (yin) currently use 1-arity. So impact is limited to future S3a drivers. Still deserves P3.\n\n6. **link-step!'s deadline scan includes `pending`** — matches spec (\"outstanding or pending entries\").\n\n7. **Return value `:expired (first overdue)`** — nil when no overdue. Also returns expired id when channel-loss! was already true before this step? Guarded by `when-not channel-gone?` → overdue is nil. Good.\n\n8. **Event ordering test** — channel-expired then append-unknown, matches spec.\n\n9. **Concurrency/step discipline in dial-step!** — mirror effects outside atom updates, tested in dial-mirror-effects-run-once-per-step. The 2-arity still runs mirror only once. Good.\n\n10. **Portability**: `some->`, `concat`, `sort-by key` — all portable. `<=` on numbers also portable. No reader conditionals needed. Catch clauses unchanged. Good.\n\n11. **Overflow/type**: give-up-after is validated as positive integer; `now` is a driver-supplied number. `+` of int+int on Dart/JS is fine. Mixed `(<= deadline now)` also fine.\n\n12. Confirming the `flood-cannot-defer-expiry` test: 60 junk values on `ba` (link's reader). drain-budget 2, step at 5 → 2 reads; step at 10 → 2 more reads, then scan finds probe id 0 with deadline 10 ≤ 10 → expires. Wait — do junk values count as reads? Yes, drain reads and discards. Probe deadline 10 (stamped at step 0 on attach… attach happens after step-at 0, and the probe is stamped at send time when new-deadline reads :now 0 → deadline 10). Good.\n\n13. `a-resend-does-not-move-the-deadline`: resend-after 1; on each `(stream/next r 0)` call: drain! → count-ask! against outstanding descriptor probe… note: probe (id 0) is outstanding, and next request is id 1. count-ask on probe re-sends probe too. No issue. Deadline of id 1 stays at 10. Step at 10 → vanishes. But wait: after `step-at ls t 9`, `stream/next r 0` — the drain inside next doesn't expire (no clock on drain). Then step at 10 expires. Test asserts `(:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? ...)`. Good.\n\n14. Another confirmation — the `resolve` path stamps deadline via send-named! and can expire; covered by `a-request-past-its-deadline...` which asserts resolve answers gone after vanishing. There's no test for expiration of a named resolve request itself (send-named!'s deadline stamp). Minor coverage gap — send-named!'s stamping path is untested (only identity path tested). P4/P3? The cond-> double new-deadline call is inside send-named! — untested. Should be caught if broken (resolve would never expire). Coverage gap: P4 recommendation to add a test where an unanswered resolve expires via :step.\n\n    Hmm, actual severity: the code path exists and appears correct. Lack of test is P4.\n\n15. `a-step-of-an-unreached-channel-is-nil` — checks nil for unreached. Good.\n\n16. `drain-budget-counts-malformed-values-and-a-gap` (S2b follow-up) — exists.\n\n17. `dial-propagates-link-options` — exists, checks give-up-after propagation.\n\n18. Confirming `:step` entry in `links` docstring — documented. `attacher` docstring mentions give-up-after. Good.\n\n19. Confirming `room?` / max-outstanding interaction with pending retention after vanishing — pending remains and counts toward room?; after vanishing, nothing is sent (guards check channel-gone?). OK.\n\n20. `link-step!` returns a map — but what if drain! set channel-gone? via end? Returns `{:channel-gone? true :expired nil}`. dial-step! then closes handle — the ring… end means the reader answered end, i.e., ring is already closed; closing handle is idempotent and correct (dial owns the connection). No issue.\n\n21. Potential issue in `dial-step!`: `stepped` is computed before mirror-step, but handle close happens after mirror-step. Even if channel vanishes, mirror step executes — responds to pending requests on the ring one last time. Harmless.\n\n22. `dial-step!`'s 1-arity: `(dial-step! dial nil)` → no `:now` recording, no link step. But establish! uses dial's `:now` — remains nil. Consistent.\n\nAlso check ws.md's Deferred section rewrite (around line 695) — confirmed, points to remote 2.4 Expiry. Good.\n\nAnother portability check: `sort-by key` over `concat` of two maps — fine in CLJS. `(first overdue)` when overdue is a vector `[id e]` — map-entry is a vector, so it works. In CLJS, map entries are vectors — yes.\n\nAlso `some->` with `(<= now)` — `<=` is a function; `(some-> d (<= now))` → `(<= d now)`. Correct.\n\nConfirm `link-step!` handles non-numeric `now` → throws in filter. Also unguarded. Merge with finding 1 (nil now) → recommend `when (some? now)` guard.\n\nFurther consideration: `link-step!` records `:now` even when channel-gone? — harmless.\n\nVerifying the author's claims about `register!` — now takes deadline parameter; `send-request!` and `refl-append` pass it. Confirmed by reading code.\n\nPotential subtle bug in `send-request!`: deadline is computed via pending lookup — but for a new probe (attach path), `send-request!` is called; on full + descriptor + room → pending entry with fresh deadline. Then retry-pending! → send-request! again → pending lookup → carries deadline. If accepted → register with that deadline, dissoc pending. Correct, no re-stamping.\n\nEdge: what if a probe that was pending (with deadline) is retried, accepted, and then answered? absorb! clears outstanding. Good.\n\nEdge: `admit-probe!` for `:probe` (waiting on reflection, unstamped): send-request! → no pending entry → new-deadline at admission time → stamped with last stepped now. Spec says stamp on admission — matches.\n\nNow, memory-leak review: outstanding cleared on vanishing; pending retained (bounded by max-outstanding if configured; unbounded if nil — but pending entries are only created via descriptor probes with room? check, and room? is unbounded when max-outstanding is nil… how many pending probes could accumulate? One per reflection (each attach probe). Reflections are created by attach — an attach storm creates unbounded reflections and probes. This is an existing S2b issue, not S2c). filed is bounded by max-filed. `:now` is scalar. No new leaks introduced by S2c, with the exception of pending retention after vanishing, but that's existing channel-loss! behavior.\n\nArithmetic: `(+ now give-up-after)` — CLJS numbers are doubles; large ms epochs (~1.7e12) + small ints are exact below 2^53. Dart int is 64-bit. Fine.\n\nConcurrency: all swap! are on driver thread per discipline; new-deadline derefs without swap — fine.\n\nOK let me assemble findings:\n\n- P3-1: `link-step!` given a nil (or non-numeric) `now` erases the link's `:now` and, if a stamped deadline exists, throws on comparison. The public `:step` entry does not guard. Unreachable via ws-project (guarded), but links is public API. Recommendation: in link-step!, `when (some? now)` guard around recording and scanning (or validate number and throw composition error).\n\n- P3-2: Requests sent before the first timed step never get a deadline — including dial compositions where `dial-attach!` runs before any `(dial-step! dial now)`. `establish!`'s step covers only when dial was previously stepped with now. Literal spec behavior (\"nothing is stamped before a step with now\"), but leaves establishment stall unbounded in an attach-first ordering — precisely the gap Expiry was meant to close. Recommendation: either document that drivers must step with now before attach, or (with architect approval) backfill deadlines at the first timed step for entries that lack them; a one-line note in docs may suffice.\n\n- P4-1: `send-named!` computes `(new-deadline link)` twice inside cond->. Pure function, but should be let-bound. Also, no test exercises the resolve request deadline/expiration path.\n\n- P4-2: `channel-loss!` leaves `:pending` entries (with deadlines) behind; harmless per sign-off, but retains reflection references until each reflection closes. Consider dissoc'ing pending in channel-loss! for hygiene (author notes this was intentionally skipped).\n\n- P4-3: `dial-step!`'s 2-arity doesn't validate that `now` is a number; a non-numeric now flows to link-step! and throws deep in comparison. (Could fold into P3-1.)\n\nPositive verifications:\n- Policy validation for give-up-after added to links (0, -1, 1.5 tested).\n- Stamping rules: stamp on accept, carry over kept probe (lookup before new-deadline — never re-stamped), waiting probes unstamped until admission, count-ask! never touches deadline, absorb! clears entry+deadline.\n- link-step!: drain first (so an answer arriving in the deadline tick clears before scan), scan skipped on channel-gone?, minimum id over outstanding+pending, event shape exact, channel-loss! after emit, return shape exact.\n- dial-step! 2-arity: projection → link step → mirror → close handle on gone; 1-arity delegates with nil; establish! steps new link with last now.\n- Portability: no host-specific code; `.-state` access in tests is pre-existing; all seq/comparison functions clj/cljs/cljd-safe; no overflow concerns.\n- Test coverage: all 7 required remote tests + 3 ws tests present, plus S2b follow-ups.\n\nVerdict: ACCEPT with non-blocking findings (P3s are robustness/documentation-level; core spec is met). Hmm — P3-2: is this within the spec's literal? Spec §1.3: \"if `:now` has not yet been recorded (driver has never stepped with now)… no deadline is stamped and nothing expires: clockless composition unchanged.\" A composition that attaches then steps with now is not clockless — it becomes clocked after the first step. The first probe remains unstamped forever. The mob's spec states deadline = \"ms since first accepted or retained send of request\" — measuring from first send requires knowing now at first send. Architect spec design rule 5: \"deadline is driver-supplied now, fixed at first send\". If now unavailable at first send, spec is silent. The author's establish! fix shows they recognized this matters in dial path. Ordering where attach precedes step: in ws_project, establish! uses dial's saved :now, so driver that steps before attach (even once) is covered. Driver that never steps before attach → probe unstamped → but subsequent dial-step! with now would… probe eventually answered or ws closed. Only pathological if peer blackholes. Given spec silence, flag as P3 and recommend documentation clarification or backfill, not blocking.\n\nVerdict: ACCEPT (with P3 follow-ups recorded for architect / S3a).\n\nNow, plan mode constraint: cannot write to collab/. Write the full findings doc to ~/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2c-review-qwen3.8-max.md and tell user we need to exit plan mode (or grant permission) to copy to the target collab path. Actually the plan file is the only permitted write. The review report is a deliverable, not a plan, but writing it there is the only option. Write the report content into the plan file, plus a one-line \"implementation step\": copy this file to collab/1791359934597-stream-s2c-review.qwen3.8-max.findings.md.\n\nBefore writing, let me double-check a few code details I want to state precisely:\n\n- In `link-step!`, when `now` is nil and no deadlines: returns map with channel-gone? and expired nil — but erases :now. Confirmed by reading code.\n\n- Verify `the-one-arity-dial-step-never-expires` asserts resolve answers retry — yes.\n\n- Verify `dial-step-with-now-closes-the-handle-on-expiry`: steps at 0 first, then attach — establish! steps link at 0 → probe stamped with deadline 10. Step at 9 → append to sock still ok (handle open). Step at 10 → expires → handle closed → append answers closed. Then :ws/closed emitted, step at 11 → projection closed, ring closed, resolve answers channel-gone.\n\nWait, one nit in that test: after handle close, `dial-resolve!` — resolve-name first drains!; ring is closed at step 11 (drain reads end → channel-loss! → gone… already gone). Then `(:channel-gone? @link)` → transformed channel-gone. Good.\n\nAlso confirming: `dial-step!` at 10 closes handle; then at 11 the projection step reads :ws/closed → closes ring → mirror-step on closed ring… mirror reads ring at its cursor; closed ring answers end → mirror returns cursor. Fine.\n\nAlso, checking whether ws handle's `stream/close!` is idempotent — sock is `(ring 8)` test fixture; ring's close! is idempotent. Fine.\n\nOne more check on `link-step!` — `(sort-by key)` over concat of outstanding/pending: both keyed by integer id. OK.\n\nAnd `emit!` before `channel-loss!`: channel-expired event precedes append-unknown events. Test verifies order. Spec says fire channel-expired then call channel-loss!. Matches.\n\nNow, write the plan/report file. Path: ~/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2c-review-qwen3.8-max.md. Wait — plan directory is ~/.commandcode/plans/. Home is /Users/sto. Use absolute path /Users/sto/.commandcode/plans/….\n\nStructure per standard template: header metadata, findings summary table, detailed analysis, verdict, and a note that we need to exit plan mode to place the file at the collab path.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I've verified the sources, tests, and docs. Let me confirm one edge case in `link-step!` before writing the report — the behavior with a nil `now`.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_847e92670f774876a8f9c949","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"defn- link-step!","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc","output_mode":"content","-A":25}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_004db949f86047d1a5afbddf","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"channel-loss!","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc","output_mode":"content","-B":8,"-A":12,"head_limit":40}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791360352959,"messageId":"259e500a-7873-4caa-bb68-f3644e6ffaf2"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_847e92670f774876a8f9c949","content":[{"type":"text","text":"src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:1105:(defn- link-step!\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1106-  \"One driver step of the link at `now`: record `now`, drain (within\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1107-   the drain budget), then, when an outstanding or kept request is\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1108-   past its deadline, emit channel-expired for the least such id and\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1109-   lose the channel (channel-loss!). The link reads no clock: `now` is\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1110-   the driver's. Answers whether the channel is gone and the id that\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1111-   expired, if any.\"\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1112-  [link now]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1113-  (swap! link assoc :now now)\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1114-  (drain! link)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1115-  (let [{:keys [outstanding pending channel-gone?]} @link\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1116-        overdue (when-not channel-gone?\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1117-                  (->> (concat outstanding pending)\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1118-                       (filter (fn [[_ e]]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1119-                                 (some-> (:deadline e) (<= now))))\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1120-                       (sort-by key)\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1121-                       first))]\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1122-    (when-some [[id e] overdue]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1123-      (emit! link {:dao.stream.remote/event\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1124-                   :dao.stream.remote/channel-expired\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1125-                   :dao.stream.remote/id id\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1126-                   :dao.stream.remote/op (:dao.stream.remote/op (:req e))})\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1127-      (channel-loss! link))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1128-    {:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? (:channel-gone? @link)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1129-     :dao.stream.remote/expired (first overdue)}))\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-1130-"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_004db949f86047d1a5afbddf","content":[{"type":"text","text":"src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-690-                (= :dao.stream/append! op)\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-691-                (emit! link v)\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-692-\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-693-                :else\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-694-                (do (file! link id req v refl)\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-695-                    (install-more! link v))))))))))\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-696-\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-697-\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:698:(defn- channel-loss!\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-699-  \"The channel reader answered end: the channel is gone. The link\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-700-   abandons its outstanding ids, reporting each abandoned append! on\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-701-   the event writer as append-unknown; filed answers are kept and\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-702-   still returned; reattachment is the caller's policy.\"\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-703-  [link]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-704-  (doseq [[id entry] (:outstanding @link)]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-705-    (when (= :dao.stream/append! (:dao.stream.remote/op (:req entry)))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-706-      (emit! link {:dao.stream.remote/event\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-707-                   :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-708-                   :dao.stream.remote/id id}))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-709-    (when-some [refl (:reflection entry)]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-710-      (swap! refl update :ids disj id)))\n--\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-754-      (when-not (and remaining (zero? remaining))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-755-        (let [r (stream/next (:reader @link) (:cursor @link))]\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-756-          (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-757-            :dao.stream/ok\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-758-            (do (absorb! link (:dao.stream/value r))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-759-                (swap! link assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-760-                (recur (when remaining (dec remaining))))\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-761-            :dao.stream/blocked nil\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:762:            :dao.stream/end (channel-loss! link)\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-763-            :dao.stream/gap\nsrc/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-764-            (do (swap! link assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r)) nil)\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-765-            nil))))))\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-766-\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-767-\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-768-;; =============================================================================\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-769-;; The reflection (2.4)\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-770-;; =============================================================================\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc-771-\n\n[Showing results with pagination = limit: 40]"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"9f6df0b5-5b6d-4f53-a448-8d665f75b7e0"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All verification items have been confirmed. Next, I'll write the review report to the plan file. Since in plan mode writing to collab/ is blocked, I'll save it to ~/.commandcode/plans/ and include steps for copying it to the target path after exiting plan mode.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Analysis complete. I'll now write the full review report. Since writing to `collab/` is blocked in plan mode, I'll save it to `~/.commandcode/plans/` and include the single step needed to place it at the target path after approval.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_16f3b6e836dc49f78bb6ad83","name":"write_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/.commandcode/plans/stream-s2c-review-qwen3.8-max.md","content":"# Plan: deliver the S2c review report\n\nThe review is complete (read-only pass over the working tree at `stream-crossmachine-s2`, base `91ab84f7`). The single remaining action, blocked by plan mode:\n\n1. Copy the report below verbatim to `collab/1791359934597-stream-s2c-review.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`.\n\nNo code changes are recommended; verdict is ACCEPT with non-blocking findings.\n\n---\n\n# S2c review: request liveness & channel expiry (qwen3.8-max)\n\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2`, branch `stream-crossmachine-s2`, base `91ab84f7`, uncommitted changes under review: `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc`, `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc`, `test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc`, `test/dao/stream/ws_project_test.cljc`, `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md`, `docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md`.\n\nRead firsthand: both source files in full, the S2c test sections of both test files, `dao.stream.remote.md` §2.4 (Channel loss + Expiry), §2.5, §3.0, `dao.stream.ws.md` Deferred/Liveness, the architect spec (`collab/1791353752491-...claude-fable-5-1.findings.md` §1.2–§4), the S2c prompt and author report (`collab/1791358000000-stream-s2c.claude-opus-5-5.*`).\n\nNote on method: plan mode blocked shell access, so `git diff 91ab84f7` was not run; the review is against the full working-tree files, with the change surface taken from the author report and cross-checked line by line against the code as it stands. Every claimed edit and test was located and verified in the files.\n\n## Finding summary\n\n| # | Severity | Location | Description | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | P3 | `remote.cljc` `link-step!` (~1112–1129) | `(step cd nil)` erases a previously recorded `:now` and, once any entry carries a `:deadline`, throws on `(<= deadline nil)` on all three hosts. Unreachable via `dial-step!` (guarded by `some? now`), but `:step` is public API of `links`. | Guard `link-step!`: when `now` is nil, skip recording and skip the scan (or throw a composition error). One `when` around the body. |\n| 2 | P3 | `remote.cljc` deadline stamping; `ws_project.cljc` `establish!` | A request that first exists on the link **before any timed step never receives a deadline**, even after the driver starts stepping with `now`. The `establish!` fix only covers dials already stepped at a `now`; a driver that attaches first and steps later leaves the attach probe (the establishment stall Expiry is meant to bound) undying. Follows the spec's letter (\"before any step with `now`, nothing is stamped\") — the spec is silent on backfill. | Record for the Architect: either document that drivers must step with `now` before the first attach/resolve, or amend the spec to backfill a deadline at the first timed step for entries that have none. Non-blocking for S2c; matters for the S3a production driver. |\n| 3 | P4 | `remote.cljc` `send-named!` | `(new-deadline link)` is computed twice in the `cond->` (test and value). Pure and single-threaded under driver discipline, so correct, but wasteful and fragile under future edits. | `(let [d (new-deadline link)] (cond-> ... (some? d) (assoc :deadline d)))`. |\n| 4 | P4 | `remote.cljc` `channel-loss!` | `:pending` (kept probes, with their deadlines and reflection refs) survives channel loss. Inert per the S2b sign-off (drain returns early once gone; `close!` clears per reflection), but a reflection that never closes retains its entry for the link's lifetime. Author flagged this and deliberately left it. | Optional hygiene: `:pending {}` in `channel-loss!`'s final `swap!`. Not required. |\n| 5 | P4 | tests | The resolve path's deadline (`send-named!` stamping → expiry of a named request through `:step`) has no dedicated test; only the post-loss `resolve` answer (`channel-gone`) is covered. | Add a small test: named request unanswered, `(step cd deadline)` expires it. |\n\n## Detailed analysis\n\n### `remote.cljc`\n\n**Policy & validation.** `:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after` is added to the `links` policy `select-keys` and to the positive-integer-or-nil validation, throwing the existing `ex-info \"invalid DaoStream remote link policy\"`. `new-link` stores `:give-up-after` and `:now nil`; the docstring is updated. Matches spec §1.2/§1.3. Tested with 0, −1, 1.5.\n\n**`new-deadline`.** `(+ now give-up-after)`, nil when either is missing. Correct clock-less default. Arithmetic is portable: JVM/CLJS/Dart all handle int+int and mixed number comparison cleanly; epoch-ms magnitudes are far below any precision boundary.\n\n**Stamping discipline.** Verified against every path:\n- Accepted sends: `register!` takes the deadline; `send-request!` and `refl-append` stamp on the writer's `ok`.\n- Kept probes: `send-request!` computes the deadline by **pending lookup first**, so a kept probe carries its original deadline through retries and its eventual accepted send — never restamped. Verified in `a-kept-probe-has-a-deadline` (retry at 5 keeps deadline 10).\n- Waiting probes (`:probe` on the reflection, S2b no-room path): unstamped until `admit-probe!` → `send-request!` finds no pending entry → stamps at admission. Correct per spec.\n- Resends: `count-ask!` touches only `:asks` — never `:deadline`. Verified by `a-resend-does-not-move-the-deadline`.\n- Answers: `absorb!`/`absorb-named!` dissoc the outstanding entry, deadline included. Verified by `an-answer-before-the-deadline-clears-it`.\n\n**`link-step!` / `:step`.** Order is exactly the spec's: record `:now`, budgeted `drain!`, then scan (skipped when `channel-gone?` is already true, so an `end`-loss in the same step doesn't double-report) for the least id in `(concat outstanding pending)` with `:deadline <= now`; emit `channel-expired` with id and op, then `channel-loss!` (which abandons outstanding, emits `append-unknown` per abandoned append, sets `channel-gone?`). Return shape `{:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? bool :dao.stream.remote/expired id-or-nil}`; nil for an unreached channel. Drain-before-scan is the right ordering: an answer arriving in the very tick the deadline passes clears its entry before the scan — no self-inflicted false loss. `:step` creating the link on a reached-but-unattached channel (via `link-for!`) is harmless and beneficial (pre-records `:now`). Findings 1 and 2 above are the only gaps.\n\n**Flood immunity.** The scan is independent of the drain budget; `flood-cannot-defer-expiry` (drain-budget 2, 60 junk values) proves the accepted D3 trade-off (possible false loss, never deferred expiry).\n\n**Memory.** No new leak: `:outstanding` is cleared on loss, `:filed`/`:filed-cursors` remain bounded by `max-filed` (S2b), `:pending` retention is pre-existing and bounded by `max-outstanding` when composed (finding 4). Expired entries cannot accumulate: expiry is a whole-channel loss, and all send paths guard on `channel-gone?`.\n\n**Portability.** No reader conditionals needed in the new code: `some->`, `concat`, `sort-by key`, `<=` on numbers, `when-some` destructuring are all clj/cljs/cljd-safe. No atom-deref side effects inside `swap!` anywhere in the new paths (`emit!`/`channel-loss!` run outside swaps). The `(<= deadline nil)` hazard of finding 1 is the only type mismatch reachable through a public entry.\n\n### `ws_project.cljc`\n\n**`dial`.** Policy `select-keys` gains `:dao.stream.remote/give-up-after`; docstring updated. Namespaced-key-only, per spec §1.2 — no bare alias. `dial-propagates-link-options` verifies all four link keys reach the link atom.\n\n**`dial-step!` multi-arity.** `([dial] (dial-step! dial nil))` preserves the existing callers exactly (verified: no `:now` recorded, no link step, nothing expires — `the-one-arity-dial-step-never-expires`). The 2-arity: records `:now` on the dial (for `establish!`), runs the budgeted projection, steps the link at `now` **between** projection and mirror (correct: freshly deposited answers reach the link before the expiry scan), then the budgeted mirror once outside any atom update (`dial-mirror-effects-run-once-per-step` still guards this), then closes the ws handle when the step answers `channel-gone? true`. `stream/closable?` check before close; ring-buffer close is idempotent, so repeated ticks after loss are safe. When `stepped` is nil (no channel / no now), the gone-check is falsy — no spurious close. The mirror still runs its last pass on the loss tick, which is benign.\n\n**`establish!`.** Stores `:cd` and `:step!`; when the dial has a recorded `now`, steps the new link at it **before** any probe exists — since `remote/links`' `:step` creates the link via `link-for!`, the later `:attach` reuses that same link with `:now` set, so the immediately-sent attach probe is stamped. This is the one behavior beyond the brief's letter; the rationale (otherwise the establishment stall goes unbounded on the dial path) is sound and the mechanism is verified against `links`/`link-for!`. Composition that never steps with `now` is untouched. Residual gap is finding 2 (attach-before-first-timed-step ordering).\n\n### Tests\n\nAll seven required `remote_test` cases and both `ws_project_test` cases exist and assert the right things, plus the two S2b follow-ups (`drain-budget-counts-malformed-values-and-a-gap`, `dial-propagates-link-options`). Highlights verified:\n- `a-request-past-its-deadline-is-channel-loss`: exact event sequence (`channel-expired` id 0 op descriptor, then `append-unknown`), exact step return maps at 9/10, outstanding emptied, `next`/`cursor`/`resolve` → `channel-gone` (not retryable), `descriptor` still `ok`.\n- `dial-step-with-now-closes-the-handle-on-expiry`: handle open at 9, closed at 10, `:ws/closed` deposit closes projection and ring, `dial-resolve!` answers `channel-gone` — the full spec §2 table row.\n- New fixtures (`stepped-links`, `attach-via`, `step-at`, `gone`, `dial-with`, `toy-cd`) are consistent with existing fixture style; `.-state` access is pre-existing in the file.\n\nCoverage gap: finding 5 (named-request expiry). Not covered by the brief's required list, so non-blocking.\n\n### Docs\n\n`dao.stream.remote.md`: §2.4 names expiry as a channel-loss cause; the new **Expiry** paragraph matches the implementation exactly (step contract, return shape, stamping rules including kept-probe carry and waiting-probe admission, resend immobility, answer clearing, clock-less default, liveness framing, flood/false-loss caveat, dial handle close). §2.5 keeps \"the reflection reads no clock\" true and adds the UDP composition note. §3.0 gains the Request-liveness bullet. `dao.stream.ws.md` Deferred/Liveness now points at remote §2.4 Expiry. No drift found between doc text and code.\n\n### Author report cross-check\n\nEvery claim in `collab/1791358000000-stream-s2c.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md` was located in the working tree, including the three \"notes for review\" (establish! beyond-brief rationale, pending retention, cosmetic test message). The reported verification runs (83 tests / 609 assertions, cljstyle clean, kondo 0 errors with the pre-existing `peer2` warning) could not be re-run in plan mode — re-running `clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.remote-test -n dao.stream.ws-project-test` is recommended before landing, per the standard workflow.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**ACCEPT.**\n\nThe slice implements the architect spec §1.2/§1.3 (S2c rows), §2 failure table, §3 test plan and §4 doc amendments faithfully; stamping, resend-immobility, answer-clearing, expiry-scan ordering and dial teardown are all correct and well-tested, with no portability or memory regressions. Findings 1–5 are non-blocking: 1 is a one-line robustness guard on a path the shipped composition never takes; 2 is a spec-silent ordering question for the Architect record and S3a's driver; 3–5 are cosmetic/hygiene. Recommend landing as one commit (`feat(dao.stream.remote): request deadlines, channel-expired and link step (cross-machine stream slice S2c)`) after re-running the test lanes, and tracking findings 1, 2 and 5 as follow-ups.\n"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791360445008,"messageId":"cc08535f-1cd1-4862-9788-2041dfb56bcd"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_16f3b6e836dc49f78bb6ad83","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Error: Tool \"write_file\" requires permissions. Use --yolo (or --dangerously-skip-permissions) to enable file writes and shell commands in print mode."}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"6a7b7a5e-e19b-4a34-97b1-5febcb98855b"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Even writing to the plan file is blocked (it seems this session is in a print mode without write permissions). Therefore, I'll submit the review directly within the response and note the intended file path. Since the deliverable is a report, I'll output it inline in its complete form.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Write permissions are blocked in this session too, so I can't save the file. The review is complete — here is the full report, ready to be saved to `collab/1791359934597-stream-s2c-review.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`:\n\n---\n\n# S2c review: request liveness & channel expiry (qwen3.8-max)\n\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2`, branch `stream-crossmachine-s2`, base `91ab84f7`, uncommitted changes under review: `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc`, `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc`, both test files, and both design docs.\n\nRead firsthand: both source files in full, the S2c test sections, `dao.stream.remote.md` §2.4 (Channel loss + Expiry), §2.5, §3.0, `dao.stream.ws.md` Deferred/Liveness, the architect spec (§1.2–§4), the S2c prompt and author report. Method note: shell and write access were blocked in this session, so `git diff` was not run; the review is against the full working-tree files, cross-checked line by line against every claim in the author report. All claimed edits and tests were located and verified.\n\n## Finding summary\n\n| # | Severity | Location | Description | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | P3 | `remote.cljc` `link-step!` (~1112–1129) | `(step cd nil)` erases a previously recorded `:now` and, once any entry carries a `:deadline`, throws on `(<= deadline nil)` on all three hosts. Unreachable via `dial-step!` (guarded by `some? now`), but `:step` is public API of `links`. | Guard `link-step!`: when `now` is nil, skip recording and skip the scan (or throw a composition error). |\n| 2 | P3 | deadline stamping; `ws_project.cljc` `establish!` | A request that first exists on the link **before any timed step never receives a deadline**, even after the driver starts stepping with `now`. The `establish!` fix covers only dials already stepped at a `now`; a driver that attaches first and steps later leaves the attach probe — the establishment stall Expiry bounds — undying. Follows the spec's letter (\"before any step with `now`, nothing is stamped\"); the spec is silent on backfill. | Record for the Architect: document that drivers must step with `now` before first attach/resolve, or amend the spec to backfill deadlines at the first timed step. Non-blocking for S2c; matters for the S3a driver. |\n| 3 | P4 | `remote.cljc` `send-named!` | `(new-deadline link)` computed twice in the `cond->`. Pure and correct under driver discipline, but fragile. | `let`-bind the deadline once. |\n| 4 | P4 | `remote.cljc` `channel-loss!` | `:pending` survives channel loss (author note 2, deliberately left). Inert per S2b sign-off, but a reflection that never closes retains its entry for the link's lifetime. | Optional hygiene: clear `:pending` in `channel-loss!`. Not required. |\n| 5 | P4 | tests | No test expires a **named** resolve request through `:step` (`send-named!`'s stamping path); only post-loss `resolve` → `channel-gone` is covered. | Add: unanswered named request, step at deadline, expiry. |\n\n## Detailed analysis\n\n**`remote.cljc` — policy.** `give-up-after` added to the `links` `select-keys` and to positive-integer-or-nil validation (`ex-info \"invalid DaoStream remote link policy\"`); `new-link` stores it plus `:now nil`. Tested with 0, −1, 1.5. Matches spec §1.2/§1.3.\n\n**`new-deadline`.** `(+ now give-up-after)`, nil when either is missing — clock-less compositions stamp nothing. Arithmetic is portable (int+int and mixed comparison are clean on JVM/CLJS/Dart; epoch-ms magnitudes are far below any precision boundary).\n\n**Stamping discipline**, verified on every path: accepted sends stamp via `register!` (`send-request!`, `refl-append`, `send-named!`); kept probes carry their original deadline because `send-request!` does the **pending lookup first** — never restamped on retry or eventual send (verified in `a-kept-probe-has-a-deadline`); waiting `:probe` entries are unstamped until `admit-probe!`; `count-ask!` touches only `:asks`, never `:deadline`; `absorb!`/`absorb-named!` remove the entry, deadline included. All exactly per spec.\n\n**`link-step!` / `:step`.** Order matches the spec: record `:now`, budgeted `drain!`, then — skipped when `channel-gone?` is already true, so an `end`-loss in the same step doesn't double-report — scan `(concat outstanding pending)` for the least id with `:deadline <= now`, emit `channel-expired` (id, op), run `channel-loss!` (abandons outstanding, `append-unknown` per append, sets gone). Return shape `{:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone? bool :dao.stream.remote/expired id-or-nil}`; nil for unreached channels. Drain-before-scan is the right ordering: an answer arriving in the deadline's own tick clears its entry before the scan. `:step` creating the link on a reached-but-unattached channel via `link-for!` is harmless and pre-records `:now`. Only gaps: findings 1–2.\n\n**Flood immunity.** The scan is independent of drain-budget; `flood-cannot-defer-expiry` (budget 2, 60 junk values) demonstrates the accepted D3 trade-off (possible false loss, never deferred expiry).\n\n**Memory.** No new leak: `:outstanding` cleared on loss; `:filed`/`:filed-cursors` bounded by `max-filed` (S2b); `:pending` retention is pre-existing and bounded by `max-outstanding` when composed (finding 4). Expired entries can't accumulate — expiry is whole-channel loss and all send paths guard on `channel-gone?`.\n\n**Portability.** No reader conditionals needed: `some->`, `concat`, `sort-by key`, numeric `<=` are clj/cljs/cljd-safe. No side effects inside `swap!` in the new paths (`emit!`/`channel-loss!` run outside swaps). The only reachable type hazard is finding 1's `(<= deadline nil)`.\n\n**`ws_project.cljc` — `dial`.** Policy `select-keys` gains `give-up-after`; namespaced-key-only per spec; `dial-propagates-link-options` verifies all four link keys land on the link atom.\n\n**`dial-step!` multi-arity.** 1-arity delegates with nil and preserves existing callers exactly (no `:now`, no link step, nothing expires — `the-one-arity-dial-step-never-expires`). 2-arity: records `:now` on the dial, budgeted projection, link step **between** projection and mirror (correct: freshly deposited answers reach the link before the expiry scan), budgeted mirror once outside any atom update (`dial-mirror-effects-run-once-per-step` still guards this), then closes the ws handle when the step answers gone — `closable?`-checked and idempotent, so repeated post-loss ticks are safe. Nil `stepped` is falsy in the gone-check — no spurious close.\n\n**`establish!`.** Stores `:cd`/`:step!` and, when the dial has a recorded `now`, steps the new link before any probe exists — since `:step` creates the link via `link-for!`, the later `:attach` reuses it with `:now` set, so the immediately-sent attach probe is stamped. This is the one beyond-the-letter behavior; the rationale is sound and the mechanism verified. Residual gap is finding 2.\n\n**Tests.** All seven required `remote_test` cases and both `ws_project_test` cases exist and assert the right things, plus the two S2b follow-ups. Highlights verified: exact event sequence in `a-request-past-its-deadline-is-channel-loss` (`channel-expired` id 0 op descriptor, then `append-unknown`), exact step return maps at 9/10, `next`/`cursor`/`resolve` → `channel-gone` not retryable, `descriptor` still `ok`; the full teardown chain in `dial-step-with-now-closes-the-handle-on-expiry` (handle open at 9, closed at 10, `:ws/closed` closes projection and ring, resolve answers `channel-gone`). Fixtures are consistent with file style. Gap: finding 5.\n\n**Docs.** §2.4 names expiry as a loss cause; the Expiry paragraph matches the implementation exactly (contract, return shape, all stamping rules, resend immobility, answer clearing, clock-less default, liveness framing, flood caveat, dial handle close). §2.5 keeps \"the reflection reads no clock\" true, adds the UDP note. §3.0 gains the Request-liveness bullet. `dao.stream.ws.md` Deferred/Liveness points at remote §2.4. No drift between doc text and code.\n\n**Author report cross-check.** Every claim located in the tree, including the three review notes. The reported runs (83 tests / 609 assertions, cljstyle clean, kondo 0 errors with the pre-existing `peer2` warning) could not be re-executed here — re-run `clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.remote-test -n dao.stream.ws-project-test` before landing.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**ACCEPT.**\n\nThe slice implements architect spec §1.2/§1.3 (S2c rows), the §2 failure table, §3 test plan and §4 doc amendments faithfully; stamping, resend immobility, answer clearing, expiry-scan ordering and dial teardown are correct and well-tested, with no portability or memory regressions. Findings 1–5 are non-blocking: 1 is a one-line robustness guard on a path the shipped composition never takes; 2 is a spec-silent ordering question for the Architect record and S3a; 3–5 are hygiene. Recommend landing as one commit (`feat(dao.stream.remote): request deadlines, channel-expired and link step (cross-machine stream slice S2c)`) after re-running the test lanes, tracking findings 1, 2 and 5 as follow-ups.\n\n---\n\nTo get this into `collab/1791359934597-stream-s2c-review.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`, re-run me with write permissions (or exit plan mode) and I'll save it."}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1791360512718,"messageId":"8738de99-7ca6-4e41-a1fb-0e7ac14a0d61"}}],"interrupted":false,"modState":{"workspace":{"cwd":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-stream-s2","platform":"darwin","branch":"stream-crossmachine-s2","gitStatus":"M docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md\n M docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md\n M src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc\n M src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc\n M test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc\n M test/dao/stream/ws_project_test.cljc\n?? collab/1791304310868-architect-transport-mob-consensus.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n?? collab/1791305896793-stream-s0-s1.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\n?? collab/1791305896793-stream-s0-s1.claude-opus-5-5.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791305896793-stream-s0-s1.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log\n?? collab/1791306950355-stream-s0-s1-review.glm-5.3.findings.md\n?? collab/1791306950355-stream-s0-s1-review.glm-5.3.prompt.md\n?? collab/1791306950355-stream-s0-s1